window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I034.upper.01","volume":1,"page":34,"physical_pdf_page":47,"layer":"upper","title":"Existence and nonexistence must already be conceived","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ضروري»؛ «فإن قولنا الشيء إما موجود أو معدوم تصديق بديهي»؛ «وأنه يتوقف على تصور الوجود والعدم فيكون بديهياً»؛ «فإن قلت إن توقفت بديهة جميع أجزائه ...»؛ «وإن زعمت أن الحكم ... بعد تصور الطرفين بديهي».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The notion of **existence** is immediately intelligible. One argument appeals to the evident truth that “a thing is either existent or nonexistent.” That judgement must involve a conception of existence and nonexistence. But the critic objects: from the evident character of the judgement it does not follow without further qualification that all its constituent conceptions are themselves primitive and immediate. Perhaps the judgement becomes evident only **after** the two sides have been conceived; its being self-evident under that condition would not prove that the conception of either side has no dependence on earlier cognition. The main text records this objection rather than silently treating the initial argument as conclusive.","source_image":"images/I034.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I035.lower.01","volume":1,"page":35,"physical_pdf_page":48,"layer":"lower","title":"Does knowing a composite entail knowing each part?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله هذا التصديق بديهي مطلقاً الخ) أي بجميع أجزائه»؛ «بداهة المجموع»؛ «بداهة كل جزء مفصلاً»؛ «الاستدلال ببداهة العلم ببداهة المجموع».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"“This judgement is self-evident without qualification” may be understood as saying that the whole composite judgement, including its constituent terms, is immediately known. The gloss denies that this is simply established: **the immediacy of the whole** cannot automatically serve as evidence for **the immediacy of each constituent part**. One can know an ordered composite under a general description without separately articulating each element's nature. Conversely, awareness of each element does not by itself yield awareness of the full arrangement. So the argument needs to specify whether the parts are taken distributively or under a collective consideration.","source_image":"images/I035.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I035.lower.02","volume":1,"page":35,"physical_pdf_page":48,"layer":"lower","title":"Avoiding a circular proof of the primitive concept","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«بديهة العلم بجهة المجموع»؛ «الجزء الأول»؛ «يلزم المصادرة»؛ «لا يستلزم المدعى»؛ «بداهة تصوّر الوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The note tests alternatives: if the self-evidence of the whole is inferred from the self-evidence of its pieces, the conclusion about one such piece is being **assumed**. If the self-evidence of each piece is inferred from that of the whole, a further rule is needed to licence the inference, and that rule has not been established merely by citing the whole. A person may possess an immediate conception of existence, but the proposed proof may fail to demonstrate that fact. The writer distinguishes the truth of the underlying claim from the sufficiency of this particular argument. [The densely printed closing comparison to the conceptual composition of a judgement still requires a line-by-line reinspection.]","source_image":"images/I035.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I035.upper.01","volume":1,"page":35,"physical_pdf_page":48,"layer":"upper","title":"The limit of the argument from immediate assent","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«كسبياً فلهذا التصديق بديهي مطلقاً ولا مصادرة»؛ «لأن بديهة مطلقاً في نفس الأمر».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The text distinguishes a judgement which is evident without inference from a knowledge which has been acquired. If the judgement is evident *in itself*, the description “self-evident” belongs to its manner of assent; it still does not follow that every preparatory conception has been established without a distinct process. The response to the charge of circularity thus depends on exactly what is being claimed as immediately known.","source_image":"images/I035.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I036.lower.01","volume":1,"page":36,"physical_pdf_page":49,"layer":"lower","title":"The cost of inferring existence from existence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وجود ضروري وهذا تصوّر وجود خاص فبداهته تستلزم بديهة العام»؛ «لكن أظهر من هذا الاستدلال ... الاستدلال على بديهة تصور العلم»؛ «(قوله ما يتصور منه كسب ... الخ)».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal commentator observes that a particular case of *existing* may be grasped immediately. Whether that establishes the immediate conception of existence **as a general notion** is another matter. He compares this argument with attempts to demonstrate the primitive intelligibility of **knowledge itself**, and argues that such demonstrations risk presupposing the conception they are meant to establish. Even when a proposition involving existence appears evident, its proof cannot be improved by confusing the immediate recognition of an instance with the logical analysis of a universal.","source_image":"images/I036.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I036.lower.02","volume":1,"page":36,"physical_pdf_page":49,"layer":"lower","title":"The relation between primitive conception and demonstrations","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أن حصول العلم بالوجود ... بديهة تصوّر العلم»؛ «الوجود»؛ «بديهي التصديق»؛ «استدلال»؛ «لا يترتب إلا بأحد الأوجه».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"An argument which says “I know that something exists” already employs both **knowledge** and **existence**; if the point is to explain how either term is acquired, it must avoid covertly using that very acquisition as a premise. The author accordingly distinguishes a *dialectical illustration* of an immediate concept from a *demonstrative derivation* of it. What can be shown is how the terms behave when used in evident judgements; one should not claim thereby to have proved every part of their intelligibility from something more primitive. The marginal note ends by bringing the discussion toward the common concept of existence on I:37. [The last line of the lower gloss is compressed and remains unverified.]\n\n# Sanandajī, *Taqrīb al-Marām* — Volume I:37–40\n## Stage 75 connected first-pass reading: common existence and the Necessary's being\n\n**Primary witness:** combined Būlāq PDF `taqribmaram(2).pdf`, SHA256 `4addd660de68794f9d64e062e3b25a2782497b3342eb4580218c3e07e8d12134`. Printed page *p* is PDF physical page *p*+13. English is a first-pass source-facing account, **not a gap-free literal translation of the entire printed lower gloss**. Arabic snippets are selected locating anchors; each lower commentary remains individually unattributed.","source_image":"images/I036.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I036.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":36,"physical_pdf_page":49,"layer":"ruled","title":"On “knowledge is not stopped”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله لا يتوقف العلم الخ) بناء على أن العلم قد يكون إجمالاً وقد يكون تفصيلاً»؛ «حصول الأول لا يتوقف على حصول الثاني»؛ «يجوز أن يجعل حصول الأول هنا ... بديهية أجزائه».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “Knowledge is not dependent, etc.”: knowledge may be **summary or general** (*ijmālī*), and it may be **detailed** (*tafṣīlī*). Possession of the former need not depend on prior possession of the latter. The author of this separate small note proposes that an immediate grasp of the whole can coexist with a later detailed apprehension of its parts, and therefore resists moving without qualification from one form of knowledge to the other. The formula at the end is an abbreviated source note, not evidence of a named writer.","source_image":"images/I036.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I036.upper.01","volume":1,"page":36,"physical_pdf_page":49,"layer":"upper","title":"Why understanding a whole does not automatically provide every part","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الأمر يتوقف على بديهة أجزائه في نفس الأمر»؛ «لكن لا يتوقف العلم ببداهته مطلقاً على العلم ببداهة أجزائه أي كل واحد منها مفصلاً»؛ «لا يتصور منه كسب ...».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"A judgement may depend, in the order of reality, on the intelligibility of its constituents; however, knowledge that the judgement itself is self-evident does **not** necessarily depend on explicitly knowing, one by one, that each constituent is self-evident. The interlocutors dispute whether this rescues the original argument or simply changes its subject. The question is a logical one about the scope of self-evidence and the status of an inferred explanation of what is evident.","source_image":"images/I036.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I036.upper.02","volume":1,"page":36,"physical_pdf_page":49,"layer":"upper","title":"The interpretation of definition","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(والتعريف له) بالكون والتحقق والشيئية»؛ «قصده تفسير مدلول اللفظ»؛ «والبداهة إنما تمنع عن التعريف الحقيقي».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The text next discusses “defining” existence through terms such as **being**, **realization**, or **thinghood**. Such words may explain what a speaker means by the word “existence,” but they do not supply an **essential real definition** proving that existence is constituted by prior components. The fact that a concept is immediate rules out a real definitional analysis of that kind; it does not prevent a **lexical clarification** or an explanation of how an unfamiliar expression is used. This point introduces the dispute over whether existence has one common meaning.","source_image":"images/I036.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I037.lower.01","volume":1,"page":37,"physical_pdf_page":50,"layer":"lower","title":"Three kinds of commonality","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الموجودات وجوه ثلاثة أحدها أن يلزم وجود أمر مشترك مع التردد في كل من الخصوصيات»؛ «فإذا نظرنا إلى وجود الممكن ... ممكناً»؛ «المعدوم»؛ «التقسيم إلى الوجود الواجب ووجود الممكن».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal author says the argument from division may be understood in more than one way. When one attends to a possible thing, one can consider its particular manner of being, and also what it shares with others; when one attends to the Necessary, its being has a different determination. The fact that we can divide both under the expression “existence” requires a common *meaning*, not necessarily a common existent genus. Moreover, the distinction between existence and nonexistence must not itself be described as another division into independently existing species of a thing. The different logical statuses of the members must be retained.","source_image":"images/I037.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I037.lower.02","volume":1,"page":37,"physical_pdf_page":50,"layer":"lower","title":"True shared meaning and verbal equivocation","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله معنى)»؛ «أن الوجود عند المتكلمين حقيقة واحدة تختلف بالإضافة إلى الماهيات المختلفة»؛ «وعند الفلاسفة حقيقة مختلفة بالذات»؛ «(قوله أو لفظي)»؛ «نسب إلى الشيخ أبي الحسن الأشعري».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “meaning”: according to one account associated here with certain theologians, existence is one common intelligible content differentiated by its relations to distinct quiddities. Philosophers, the gloss reports, may instead understand particular modes of existence to differ in reality while sharing an abstract predicate. The author distinguishes such positions from the thesis that the term “existence” is **merely homonymous**. He reports an attribution of a stronger difference in the meaning of existence to al-Ashʿarī and asks how it is to be understood, rather than simply accepting that the two doctrines are equivalent. The closing remark on what it means to “join the special to the common” goes on to I:38.","source_image":"images/I037.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I037.upper.01","volume":1,"page":37,"physical_pdf_page":50,"layer":"upper","title":"Shared meaning and division","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أن اشتراك الموجودات في الوجود معنوي أو لفظي»؛ «بأنه على اشتراك معنوي»؛ «وجود (الواجب) ووجود (غيره)»؛ «التقسيم المشترك لا يقال للوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The question is whether the things said to exist share the word “existence” merely by homonymy or share an intelligible **meaning**. The position defended here is that existence is said of them through a common meaning, though the particular modes of existence differ. The text identifies several ways to analyze this distinction and warns that the common meaning should not be confused with one numerically identical extramental entity distributed among individuals. The Necessary and what is other than it must both fall under the relevant expression if the division is to be intelligible.","source_image":"images/I037.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I037.upper.02","volume":1,"page":37,"physical_pdf_page":50,"layer":"upper","title":"The threefold division","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(بنيه على اشتراك معنوي)»؛ «(صح التقسيم إلى) وجود (الواجب) ووجود (غيره)»؛ «وجود القسمة مشترك بين جميع أقسامها»؛ «كالجوهر والعرض».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The proof takes the form of a valid **division**: existence is divided into the existence of the Necessary and the existence of others, and the latter admits further divisions, such as substance and accident. A division requires that the divided term be intelligibly common to its members; it does not prove that the thing divided exists as a separate reality alongside all the members. The distinction between a logical common notion and an independent common entity is therefore crucial.","source_image":"images/I037.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I038.lower.01","volume":1,"page":38,"physical_pdf_page":51,"layer":"lower","title":"No external universal is established by common predication","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله بل كان له في كل معنى الخ)»؛ «على تقدير أن يكون مشتركا لفظيا»؛ «خصوصية مختلفة»؛ «المفهوم»؛ «مطلق الماهية والتشخص».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss differentiates the sharing of an **expression** from the sharing of an intelligible content. A single word may designate entirely different objects, but then it does not provide the common content needed for the proposed division. Conversely, when one grasps the common content of “existence,” one has not thereby established a universal existence outside the mind, over and above the particular existents. The author asks whether specialization is something added in the concept or something constituting each existing thing as that thing. Merely treating particularity as an accident of an independently existing universal would beg the question.","source_image":"images/I038.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I038.lower.02","volume":1,"page":38,"physical_pdf_page":51,"layer":"lower","title":"Two questions hidden in “existence”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله ما اتصف بالوجود)»؛ «(قوله فأي مفهوم من معانيه)»؛ «الوجود بأحد المعاني»؛ «المعنى الاعتباري»؛ «الإنسان».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “whatever is qualified by existence”: the question is **which sense** of existence is being predicated. If one means a mental or relational meaning, this can be commonly conceived; if one means the reality by which the individual is actual, it does not follow that every individual actualizes one numerically identical external being. The gloss contrasts those possibilities through the example of “human being” and asks which predicate makes it proper to say that a person exists rather than does not. The final objection continues onto I:39.","source_image":"images/I038.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I038.upper.01","volume":1,"page":38,"physical_pdf_page":51,"layer":"upper","title":"What is shared and what differs","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لا تتوقف على الوضع والعلم ولذلك لا تختلف باختلاف اللغات»؛ «مع الاشتراك اللفظي»؛ «(الأمر الثاني)»؛ «(يلزم مع التردد في الخصوصية)».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"What is shared in meaning is not established simply by a convention of language; cognition of existence, on this argument, is not confined to the accidental distinctions among languages. Nevertheless, the fact that a term is used in several cases does not by itself prove that those cases contain one and the same external entity. The second argument considers the intelligibility of differences among the **specializations** of existence and the possibility of reflecting on them while retaining a general notion.","source_image":"images/I038.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I038.upper.02","volume":1,"page":38,"physical_pdf_page":51,"layer":"upper","title":"Possible, necessary and nonexistent","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(تمام الحصر في الموجود والمعدوم)»؛ «ولو لم يكن الوجود مشتركا معنويا»؛ «لزم الحصر في قولنا الإنسان إما موجود أو معدوم»؛ «المعدوم».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"An exhaustive contrast such as “the human being is either existent or nonexistent” presupposes that we understand the negation being made. The opponent cannot explain that opposition solely by ascribing unrelated meanings to “existence” in the cases to which it applies. The text also considers the division of what exists into Necessary and possible. But this reasoning must be handled carefully: the terms of a division can be intelligible in the mind even where not every term names something actual outside it.","source_image":"images/I038.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I039.lower.01","volume":1,"page":39,"physical_pdf_page":52,"layer":"lower","title":"What does the denial actually deny?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله يجوز الاتصاف بالوجود بمعنى آخر الخ)»؛ «مفهوم العدم»؛ «الوجود والماهية هو تغايرهما بحسب المفهوم دون الهوية»؛ «(قوله أي عن الماهية الخ)»؛ «سلب مبدا الاشتقاق».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The lower commentator says the argument depends on **what it is that is denied**. To say that a possible quiddity is nonexistent is not to deny that the concept of that quiddity can be thought, nor to place an external thing called “nonexistence” where actual existence should be. The difference in meaning between existence and quiddity may warrant denying one predicate of the other in thought, without proving an external quiddity lacking existence. The gloss distinguishes denial of a **derived predicate** from denial of the underlying source or principle of the predicate, and asks whether the inference from the former to the latter is licensed.","source_image":"images/I039.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I039.lower.02","volume":1,"page":39,"physical_pdf_page":52,"layer":"lower","title":"Necessary and possible existence remain different","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الفرق بين معنى الوجود والماهية»؛ «سلب الوجود عن الماهية»؛ «المعنى المصدري الانتزاعي»؛ «المحقق»؛ «الوجود المقابل للعدم».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The writer returns to the distinction between the abstract verbal or intellectual meaning of existence and the actuality that stands opposite nonexistence. If existence is understood in the abstract derivative sense, it can be conceptually set aside; doing so does not itself establish that the thing's concrete reality is removed or that the thing remains externally real in its absence. If the sense is actual existence, the matter is different. The objection is directed against sliding between these senses while asserting that one and the same entity can be both present and absent. [The final sentence runs into the causal argument on I:40.]","source_image":"images/I039.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I039.upper.01","volume":1,"page":39,"physical_pdf_page":52,"layer":"upper","title":"A shared meaning does not prove that every nonexistent is a thing","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«يجوز الاتصاف بالوجود بمعنى آخر فلم يكن معدوما ولا موجودا بالمعنى الذي أريد»؛ «(على زيادته على الماهية ذهنا)»؛ «أن العقل يلاحظ الوجود دون الماهية»؛ «ثم الوجود له (عنها)».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"It is possible for one and the same thing to be spoken of under different senses of “existent”; this does not mean that a thing is at once existent and nonexistent *in the same respect*. An argument that existence is **additional to quiddity in the mind** starts from the intellect's ability to consider existence without considering the particular quiddity, and vice versa. This difference of conception is not yet a proof of two extramental realities.","source_image":"images/I039.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I039.upper.02","volume":1,"page":39,"physical_pdf_page":52,"layer":"upper","title":"Two arguments for mental distinction","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(الأول)»؛ «(صح تسلبه) أي الوجود (عنها)»؛ «أي عن الماهية»؛ «فلو كان عينها لما جاز ذلك لامتناع سلب الشيء عن نفسه»؛ «(و) الثاني».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"One argument is that the mind can **deny existence** of a quiddity: a quiddity can be conceived as nonexistent. If existence were identical with the quiddity in every sense, denying it would seem to be denying the thing of itself, which is impossible. The second argument appeals to the distinction between what the mind understands by the quiddity and what it understands by existence; the significance of that distinction is considered across I:39–40. The upper stream is explicit that the thesis under investigation concerns **addition in intellect**, not necessarily an added external accident.","source_image":"images/I039.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I040.lower.01","volume":1,"page":40,"physical_pdf_page":53,"layer":"lower","title":"Does giving existence cause a regress?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله لأن جعل الشيء على نفسه الخ)»؛ «الحمل هنا أيضا هو الحمل الاشتقاقي»؛ «لا حمل المواطأة»؛ «حقيقة الوجود ...»؛ «الوجود الزيادة».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal writer says the alleged self-predication is not always a case of strict identity: the predication in question may be **derivative**, not a claim that the subject and predicate are one in every respect. A quiddity's being qualified by existence is therefore not exactly the same sort of proposition as the quiddity's being identical with itself. Yet if one makes a distinct extramental “existence” into a further thing, it must in turn have existence and the regress returns. The distinction must be located at the level of explanation rather than by multiplying external entities.","source_image":"images/I040.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I040.lower.02","volume":1,"page":40,"physical_pdf_page":53,"layer":"lower","title":"Why the possible is not thereby Necessary","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لزم احتياجه إلى غيره والاحتياج إلى غيره ممكن»؛ «الماهية لا يمكن أن تكون من غير الوجود ...»؛ «وإذ ذاك يكون وجوده نفس الماهية»؛ «فيكون المعنى ...».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss presses the objection: if a possible thing's “existence” is merely an intellectual addition, why does it still require an efficient cause, whereas the Necessary does not? The reply must distinguish **dependence on an external cause** from dependence on an independently existing accident named existence. An object whose actuality is given by another is possible, even though its quiddity and concrete existence are not two separately actual items. The Necessary is understood as having no such external causal dependence. The author notes that a purely terminological answer will not suffice; he proceeds to the problem of the Necessary's quiddity and the causal priority of essence in I:41.\n\n# Sanandajī, *Taqrīb al-Marām* — Volume I, printed pp. 41–48\n## Stage 75 — connected Arabic–English source-reading, including the marginal register\n\n**Witness:** the user's combined, image-only Būlāq print `taqribmaram(2).pdf`, SHA-256 `4addd660de68794f9d64e062e3b25a2782497b3342eb4580218c3e07e8d12134`. Printed page I:*p* = PDF physical page *p*+13. **Status:** first-pass, source-facing translations; the Arabic quotations below are **locating anchors, not an exhaustive diplomatic transcription**. The independently printed lower commentary is of **unassigned individual authorship**; only explicit evidence can establish a particular author's identity. Brackets indicate translator-supplied clarification or uncertainty. This supplement does **not** certify any page as line-exhaustively translated or independently proofread.","source_image":"images/I040.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I040.upper.01","volume":1,"page":40,"physical_pdf_page":53,"layer":"upper","title":"Causation presupposes priority","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(إفادة محله عليها)»؛ «ولو كان عينها لأفاد المحل الشيء على نفسه»؛ «(و) الثالث (اكتساب ثبوتها)»؛ «يقتقر إلى كسب الوجود»؛ «ثبوت الماهية».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The third argument concerns the quiddity's acquiring the establishment or actuality which it lacks in itself. If existence were simply the quiddity, would not the cause be causing the thing **as itself**, a peculiar causal self-reference? The argument appeals to the way the thing acquires existence; the analysis of what it is for a cause to confer existence must distinguish a thing's receptive capacity and an efficient agent. The text invokes the notion of “acquiring” being, but it does not follow that a pre-existing quiddity must already occupy the external world as its own causal recipient.","source_image":"images/I040.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I040.upper.02","volume":1,"page":40,"physical_pdf_page":53,"layer":"upper","title":"The theologians' response concerning the Necessary","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«واستدل بها ... اختلاف ثبوت الماهية»؛ «(والحكم على أن حقيقة الواجب وجوده خاص)»؛ «فالوجود عندهم نفس الماهية في الواجب»؛ «قالوا لو كان الوجود في الواجب زائدا على الماهية».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The theologians have been represented as holding that the reality of the Necessary is its **special existence**. On that interpretation, in the Necessary existence is not additional to a separate quiddity. They argue: if its existence were an added feature of a quiddity, it would depend on something other than that quiddity, which would destroy the claim that it is necessary through itself. The discussion thus turns on whether necessity is explained by the thing's actual being or by an additional attribute that itself needs explanation. The last sentence joins I:41.","source_image":"images/I040.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I041.lower.01","volume":1,"page":41,"physical_pdf_page":54,"layer":"lower","title":"The distinction between a possible thing and its existence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«متغايرين بالمفهوم والاعتبار لكنهما في الخارج موجود له هوية واحدة»؛ «الإمكان الذاتي للوجوب»؛ «غير محتاج إلى غير ذاته»؛ «اتصاف الذات بالوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"A quiddity and its existence can differ in concept and mode of consideration while having a **single identity externally**. There is then no further item outside the mind by whose addition the thing is first made an existent. The gloss warns that an argument from possible existence to necessary existence must not assume the very external duality that is under dispute. If possibility attaches to a thing considered through itself, this does not mean a real quiddity is already present outside the mind, waiting for an extramental accident called existence. Likewise, necessary existence through itself must not be described as though a separately actualized existence were attached to a preceding subject. The discussion concerns how our concepts are applied to what is necessary and what is possible, and whether the proposed distinction is merely intellectual or requires a second actual thing.","source_image":"images/I041.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I041.lower.02","volume":1,"page":41,"physical_pdf_page":54,"layer":"lower","title":"A question about the priority required of causes","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الممكن الذاتي»؛ «الواجب ما يحتاج في ثبوت الوجود له إلى غير ذاته» [read against surrounding lines: **the exact negation/wording must be checked**]؛ «(قوله تقدم ماهية الممكن الخ)»؛ «القابلة للوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss further contrasts the possible thing, whose existence is dependent on something else, with that which exists by itself. It distinguishes the priority of an efficient cause from the status of a thing capable of receiving existence: the receptacle cannot simply be treated as an independently existent cause of that same existence. The margin's compressed formulation of the relation between what is necessary through itself and what is dependent on an efficient cause still needs a word-for-word second proof; the distinction is recorded without supplying a fictitious variant.","source_image":"images/I041.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I041.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":41,"physical_pdf_page":54,"layer":"ruled","title":"Separate short note","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله هو العلة الفاعلة) دون القابلة بناء على أن قيامه بالماهية في الممكن إنما هو ذهناً لا خارجاً كما يأتي اه منه».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “It is the efficient cause”: [this is] in contrast to a receptive cause, based on the position that existence subsists in a possible quiddity only **mentally**, not as an additional, separately inhering item outside the mind, as will be explained. “From him” is a printed attributional formula but does not by itself give a personal name.","source_image":"images/I041.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I041.upper.01","volume":1,"page":41,"physical_pdf_page":54,"layer":"upper","title":"Main text, beginning in I:40","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الواجب في وجوده محتاجاً إلى غيره فلا يكون الواجب واجباً»؛ «أن تكون نفس الماهية والعلة متقدمة بالوجود على المعلول»؛ «فيلزم تقدم الماهية بالوجود على الوجود وذلك محال»؛ «وأجيب بأن العلة متقدمة ...».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"[Were the Necessary to depend for its existence on something other than itself,] the Necessary would be dependent on another and would not be necessary [in itself]. Another alternative is that the very quiddity acts as the cause. But a cause precedes its effect with respect to existence, so this would entail that the quiddity exists prior to its own existence, an impossibility. The reply distinguishes the sort of priority required here. It is the *efficient* cause that must precede the effect in existence, not every condition or receptacle in which existence may be said to obtain. [The text turns to how the possible's quiddity is conceived as a subject for existence; the last clause runs onto I:42.]","source_image":"images/I041.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I042.lower.01","volume":1,"page":42,"physical_pdf_page":55,"layer":"lower","title":"Why an efficient cause must be conceived as existent","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ما لم يلاحظ كون الشيء موجوداً امتنع أن يلاحظ مبدأ الوجود ومفيداً له»؛ «بخلاف القابل للوجود»؛ «وإلا لزم حصول الحاصل»؛ «ولا العدم وإلا لزم اجتماع النقيضين».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Before the intellect can consider a thing as the **source or giver of existence**, it must consider that thing as existent. A thing understood merely as receptive of existence is different: it must not be presumed already to have the very existence that it is supposed to receive, for that would amount to bringing about what is already realized. Nor can one simply put it under nonexistence while ascribing to it the standing of an actually existent subject; that risks combining contradictories. The gloss is concerned with the exact stage and sense in which something is called a “subject,” “cause,” or “receptacle,” not the crude claim that a nonexistent thing performs a causal act.","source_image":"images/I042.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I042.lower.02","volume":1,"page":42,"physical_pdf_page":55,"layer":"lower","title":"The distinction between an *iʿtibārī* predicate and an external constituent","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الماهية على التقدير المذكور تصير علة اعتبارية لا خارجية»؛ «القائلين بزيادته»؛ «أقول فتقرر في المباحث السابقة»؛ «الاتحاد في المفهوم»؛ «مغايرة له من حيث المفهوم».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On this account, the priority assigned to quiddity for the purpose of predicating existence is a priority **within consideration**, not the priority of an independently present extramental efficient cause. Thus the common assertion that existence is “additional to quiddity” must be situated within a precise theory of what the addition means. It need not assert two separate things outside the mind. The marginal critic presses the question of how this can be reconciled with the philosophers' account of the unique Necessary, and with the many possible things whose existence is spoken of in a shared manner. When the Necessary's existence is called identical with its quiddity, this is not simply saying that the *concept* of existence and the *concept* of quiddity are indistinguishable; the argument concerns the reality indicated by each.","source_image":"images/I042.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I042.upper.01","volume":1,"page":42,"physical_pdf_page":55,"layer":"upper","title":"The argument and its answer","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«تقدير إمكانه يحتاج إلى فاعل فيلزم المحال»؛ «لكن الوجود عند القائلين بزيادته أمر اعتباري لا يحتاج إلى موجود فلا إشكال»؛ «قائم بنفسه متميز من غيره مخالف لوجود الممكن في حقيقته»؛ «مع تفرده ... بالقيام بالماهية».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"If the added existence itself had to be a possible existing entity, it would require an agent and the difficulty would recur. But those who say existence is additional [to quiddity] take that addition, in the relevant sense, to be **an intellectual consideration**, not an independently existing entity requiring its own further existence. So the objection does not arise in that form. The Necessary's existence is described as subsisting through itself, distinct from what is other than it and different in reality from the existence of the possible. This is so despite the existence of the possible being conceived as standing in a quiddity. The relevant distinction is between the unique self-subsistence of the Necessary and the possible's mode of dependence, not simply between two things represented by the same universal concept.","source_image":"images/I042.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I042.upper.02","volume":1,"page":42,"physical_pdf_page":55,"layer":"upper","title":"What abstraction cannot mean","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«مع تفرده»؛ «بالقيام بالماهية»؛ «لأن مقتضى الذات الذي لا يتخلف عنه»؛ «فيكون وجود الممكن مجرداً أيضاً وهو باطل»؛ «فلا يرد أن تجرد الوجود عن الماهية ...».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"One must not say that existence, considered through its own nature, is necessarily abstracted from every quiddity; whatever follows essentially from a nature would then accompany it in every case. The possible's existence too would have to be independent of quiddity, contrary to the very contrast being drawn. The possible quiddity, as possible in itself, admits nonexistence; its possibility is not abolished merely by abstractly conceiving its existence. [The upper discourse continues into I:43.]","source_image":"images/I042.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I043.lower.01","volume":1,"page":43,"physical_pdf_page":56,"layer":"lower","title":"Does denying extramental addition deny the quiddity itself?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ضرورة توقف وجوده على تجرده»؛ «التجرد ... عن المقارنات»؛ «قائم به في الخارج»؛ «أن ما هو في الخارج زيادة عليه خارجاً معنى تنافيهما»؛ «المعروض له ليس في الخارج شيء واحد».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal commentator tests the assertion that a quiddity “subsists” and that existence stands in it. A requirement that the thing be separated or individuated does not by itself prove a **second independent being** alongside the individuated thing. If the reality is one outside the mind, the two analyses—“quiddity” and “existence”—may designate it under different considerations without being two substances or an accident and a substance. The critic asks whether the proposed explanation surreptitiously makes one of those conceptual factors actual before the other. Such an account would fail to explain how a single existent has the identity it does.","source_image":"images/I043.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I043.lower.02","volume":1,"page":43,"physical_pdf_page":56,"layer":"lower","title":"The whiteness-and-body analogy","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن كان المعروض له ليس في الخارج شيء واحد يطلق عليه البياض والجسم»؛ «بل شيئان كل منهما متحقق على حدة»؛ «لكن لا تسلم إن الماهية مع الوجود بعد عروضها لها ...»؛ «فلا يلزم ما ذكر».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"When a body is white, whiteness and body are not merely two interchangeable names for one individual in the way under dispute: they are distinct grounds of predication, each considered as having its own mode of realization. But one cannot simply transfer this relation to quiddity and existence. The gloss insists on distinguishing the concept of what the thing is from the concept of its being, without inferring that its existence must be another extramental item attached to an already actual quiddity. The analogy therefore needs a careful qualification. [The long final clause continues on I:44.]","source_image":"images/I043.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I043.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":43,"physical_pdf_page":56,"layer":"ruled","title":"Separate short objection","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله إذ لا يتقرر الخ) بناء على أنه ليس الهوية المتحدة مع الماهية في الخارج لأنه أمر اعتباري لا تحقق له في الخارج كما هو عند المتكلمين فافهم اه منه».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “Since it is not separately established, etc.”: this proceeds on the assumption that the alleged additional existence is not itself the one external identity united with the quiddity, for it is an intellectual consideration with no separate external realization, as on the theologians' account. Understand. [The note ends with a formula of derivation, not a verified personal signature.]","source_image":"images/I043.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I043.upper.01","volume":1,"page":43,"physical_pdf_page":56,"layer":"upper","title":"The special existence is not the universal concept","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فلا يكون واجباً ولا راداً بأن الوجود معلوم بالضرورة وحقيقة الواجب غير معلومة اتفاقاً»؛ «البحث ليس في الوجود المشترك بل في الوجود الخاص المخالف لسائر الوجودات في الحقيقة»؛ «والمجرد هو الثاني والمعلوم بالضرورة هو الأول».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"[If the possible's existence were separated from quiddity in the same manner,] it would not thereby become necessary, and the objection that “existence is necessarily known, whereas the reality of the Necessary is agreed to be unknown” does not reach the intended point. The discussion is **not about existence as a concept shared by many things**. It concerns the *special existence* differing in reality from all other existences. The existence described as independent or separated [from quiddity] is the latter; the existence grasped necessarily is the former, common notion. They must not be interchanged in the objection.","source_image":"images/I043.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I043.upper.02","volume":1,"page":43,"physical_pdf_page":56,"layer":"upper","title":"What “inhering in quiddity” signifies","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ذهناً لا عيناً»؛ «إذ لا يتقرر كل متحقق على حدة في الخارج»؛ «كبياض الجسم»؛ «فإنه قائم في الخارج»؛ «فلا يلزم أن تقوم الماهية ...».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The possible's existence is said to subsist in quiddity **in the mind, not as two separately actualized things outside it**. One must not picture the quiddity as first possessing an independent extramental existence, and then receiving the accident of existence, like the colour white borne by a body. The illustrative analogy with whiteness is thus qualified. It is the mind that distinguishes what it conceives as the thing and as its being; outside the mind there is no need to posit a second, already existent quiddity that serves as a host for an equally independent existence. “The existence of everything is additional to it ...” passes into the next leaf.","source_image":"images/I043.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I044.lower.01","volume":1,"page":44,"physical_pdf_page":57,"layer":"lower","title":"The priority of quiddity and the Necessary's existence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الفرق الظاهر بين الصورتين هو أن قيام البياض بالجسم مسبوق بوجود الجسم بخلاف قيام الوجود بالماهية»؛ «الوجود الواجبي عين الماهية»؛ «معنى كون وجود الواجب نفسه»؛ «غير محتاج إلى وجود آخر».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The obvious difference between the two cases is that whiteness comes to stand in a body whose existence is already presupposed. The supposed standing of existence in a quiddity cannot be treated in the same manner. For the Necessary, saying that existence is its very reality is intended to deny that its being depends on another existence. If every actual existence had to be supplied by some further existence, no existence would ever be explained. It is therefore no objection merely to say that we distinguish the notion of the Necessary's essence from our notion of existence; the point is whether the being of that essence requires some second actual thing.","source_image":"images/I044.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I044.lower.02","volume":1,"page":44,"physical_pdf_page":57,"layer":"lower","title":"A critical test of the explanation","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«إنه غير محتاج إلى وجود آخر يقوم به»؛ «لا معنى للواجب سوى ما يكون تحققه بنفسه»؛ «كان الممكن ... لم يفتقر إلى وجود آخر»؛ «المصنف في شرح المقاصد»؛ «هذا المذهب إلى الضلال البعيد».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"A thing whose existence is through itself does not require a further being to make it actual; that is how the claim about the Necessary is explained. But the marginal writer then presses a difficulty: if “being by itself” merely means not needing a separately added existence in the same way as a possible thing does not require a new extramental item called its existence, the proposed account risks losing the difference between what is necessary and what is possible. The possible still depends on a cause; the Necessary does not. The critic invokes the author's discussion in *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* and contests the attribution of a particular metaphysical thesis to it. The concluding polemical expression about a doctrinal view being “far astray” is **reported as the marginal writer's claim**, not endorsed here. [Some compressed words before the final formula require checking against a clearer image or second print.]","source_image":"images/I044.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I044.upper.01","volume":1,"page":44,"physical_pdf_page":57,"layer":"upper","title":"The end of the rebuttal","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وأيضاً الماهية المعروضة إما معدومة فمتناقض أو موجودة فدور أو تسلسل»؛ «ليس كقيام البياض بالجسم»؛ «وجود الواجب له ...»؛ «والوجود المشترك ...»؛ «بالتشكيك».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"If the quiddity supposedly receiving existence were **nonexistent**, the position would be contradictory; if **already existent**, the argument would move in a circle or require an endless regress. Hence the addition of existence is not to be understood exactly as whiteness inhering in a body. The Necessary has its special existence, while the common notion of existence can be predicated across things that are not identical in their modes of realization. It is in this setting that the text speaks of the common predicate and of gradational predication (*tashkīk*). The marginal discussion asks what kind of unity this common predicate has, rather than presupposing a single external genus of existents.","source_image":"images/I044.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I044.upper.02","volume":1,"page":44,"physical_pdf_page":57,"layer":"upper","title":"The distinction between the shared predicate and its realization","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«هو الوجود المشترك المقول على الوجودات الخاصة (بالتشكيك)»؛ «المحلول في الجوهر أو ...»؛ «كالبياض ... كالحركة»؛ «فالوجود عارض الماهية ... بالتشكيك».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The existence predicated of particular existences is a **common meaning**, predicated with gradation. If it is spoken of as something “inhering,” the intended inherence varies with the category under consideration; comparison with whiteness and with motion should not be confused with a proof that every particular existence is itself a separately existing recipient of an accident called *being*. The analogy is a way of explaining shared predication despite different instances, and the precise force of “gradation” remains part of the ensuing controversy.","source_image":"images/I044.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I045.lower.01","volume":1,"page":45,"physical_pdf_page":58,"layer":"lower","title":"The nominal character of the common predicate","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله جاز كون واحد من الوجودات الخاصة الخ)»؛ «ما ثبت من اشتراك الكل في مفهوم الوجود لا يقتضي تصادق أفراده»؛ «كأفراد الماني من أنواع الحيوانات»؛ «الوجود المطلق صادق على الوجودات الخاصة المختلفة الحقائق».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss objects that sharing in the **concept of existence** does not require distinct actual existences to coincide or be identical. The common term “animal” can be said of many living kinds without those species becoming one external thing; so “existence,” too, may be said of existences different in their particular realities. But one must distinguish a concept shared by things from a single actual entity shared among them. In such cases, what is common is the mode of predication, not an additional piece of reality lying between the individuals.","source_image":"images/I045.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I045.lower.02","volume":1,"page":45,"physical_pdf_page":58,"layer":"lower","title":"Existence, the subject, and the universal","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الحقيقة الواحدة صادقة على الوجودات الخاصة المختلفة الحقائق»؛ «التواطؤ»؛ «التشكيك»؛ «الموجود»؛ «الوجود لا الوجودات»؛ «كأفراد المفهوم».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"There remains a difficulty if the particular existences are treated as radically different realities and the shared predicate “existence” as a real genus. What is supposed to be common is not literally the same as what individuates each thing. The note asks whether predication here is univocal (*tawāṭuʾ*), gradational (*tashkīk*), or only common in name. The distinction between the concept of “what exists” and the existence of the individual is important: the former is a mental universal, while the latter is the actuality of that individual. The writer then considers the opponent's suggestion that establishing the particular existences requires an additional “being” and rejects inferring a separate accident simply from this language. The argument continues on I:46.","source_image":"images/I045.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I045.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":45,"physical_pdf_page":58,"layer":"ruled","title":"What the upper text means by “establishment”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله بمعنى الثبوت) مع أن الظاهر أن جعله عليه لا يكون إلا بهذا»؛ «لفظ الوجود مشتركاً لفظياً»؛ «جعل الكون على الوجودات».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On “in the sense of establishment”: the marginal writer notes that the interpretation appears unavoidable if the term is to retain a meaning across particular instances. Otherwise “existence” might become a merely equivocal word, with no conceptual unity. But interpreting it this way makes the relation of predicate and subject problematic. A verbal or conceptual distinction alone does not amount to one real item supervening on another.","source_image":"images/I045.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I045.upper.01","volume":1,"page":45,"physical_pdf_page":58,"layer":"upper","title":"The nature of gradational predication","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فهو له المقول على الوجودات ... بمنزلة العلة»؛ «ليكون الكون عارضاً»؛ «ولا يلزم من ذلك تكثر الوجودات الخاصة»؛ «لأن زيادة العارض لا تستلزم زيادة المعروض».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The predicate common to the particular existences is spoken of according to a gradational or derivative predication. On the account in question, *kawn*—the fact of being—is treated as an accident. It does not follow from its being predicated of many existents that the individual existences themselves multiply in exactly the same way, for multiplicity of the predicate or accident does not entail multiplicity of what bears it. The particular existences are each distinguished in their realities; this is the problem that the common concept has to account for.","source_image":"images/I045.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I045.upper.02","volume":1,"page":45,"physical_pdf_page":58,"layer":"upper","title":"The accident–substrate difficulty","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن كون واحد من الوجودات الخاصة قائماً بنفسه»؛ «لكن لا يخفى أن الوجود الخاص لكل شيء»؛ «فلا يرد أنه لو كان ... فلا يصح جعل الكون بمعنى الثبوت عليهم»؛ «كالنور على الأنوار».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"If the particular existence of a thing means nothing over and above its **actual establishment**, then saying that being inheres in it risks making the predicate and its subject the same thing under two descriptions. That difference of consideration is insufficient for a real accident standing in a real substrate. If particular existence is a reality besides actual establishment, however, the proposal that *kawn* means establishment when predicated of it becomes obscure. The analogy, “as light is predicated of lights,” cannot simply settle the question; the comparison is continued on I:46.","source_image":"images/I045.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I046.lower.01","volume":1,"page":46,"physical_pdf_page":59,"layer":"lower","title":"How “existence” is used","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وجود الوجود لفظاً»؛ «حمل الخلاف في الوجود»؛ «المعنى النسبي»؛ «الماهية»؛ «مبدأ الآثار»؛ «المشتق ... مشتق معنوي أو لفظي».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal writer examines the possibility that the disagreement about the addition of existence is in part **verbal**. Those who assert an addition may be speaking of the relational or mental meaning of existence, whereas those who say existence and quiddity are identical mean the concrete reality which grounds effects. The note then asks whether the common predicate is a genuine conceptual derivative, or only a linguistic extension; how can it be predicated of existences which are supposed to differ in reality? The answer cannot be obtained simply by saying that the word “existence” occurs in both accounts. The sense of the predicate must be specified.","source_image":"images/I046.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I046.lower.02","volume":1,"page":46,"physical_pdf_page":59,"layer":"lower","title":"What follows from the attribution to al-Ashʿarī","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فيعارض النور المطلق)»؛ «(قوله كأنه لهذا قال المعدوم الخ)»؛ «المصنف في شرح المقاصد»؛ «العدم ممتنع على القول بزيادة الوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"On the analogy with unrestricted light, the critic wonders whether the general predicate is superadded to individual lights in external reality, or belongs to them as something the intellect understands. On the suggestion that al-Ashʿarī denied the thinghood of the nonexistent: this may follow if existence is the very reality of the thing; but it does not follow simply from a verbal definition. The note appeals to the author's discussion elsewhere in *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, and points out that some interlocutors affirm the **possibility of nonexistent quiddities being things** precisely because they regard existence as additional. The detailed inference continues on I:47.","source_image":"images/I046.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I046.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":46,"physical_pdf_page":59,"layer":"ruled","title":"Distinguishing external reality and an extracted concept","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«تلك الوجودات الخاصة موجودة بحقيقة لأن لازم ...»؛ «الوجود حقيقة فيها هو أثر الفاعل في الممكنات وعين الذات في الواجب تعالى»؛ «الكون العارض إنما هو بطريق التجوز»؛ «أمر اعتباري انتزعه العقل».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Those particular existences are actual in virtue of their individual reality: in possible things, the reality of existence is the effect of an efficient cause; in the Necessary, it is identical with the essence. To call an intellectually extracted predicate—*kawn* or “being”—an additional existing accident is, on this account, to speak figuratively. The intellect has drawn out a feature from what is genuinely existent. The writer emphasizes that reality as the **principle of effects** must be distinguished from an abstracted consideration to which a similar name is applied. [The small final connective phrase in the ruled note requires a second literal reading.]","source_image":"images/I046.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I046.upper.01","volume":1,"page":46,"physical_pdf_page":59,"layer":"upper","title":"The light analogy","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أنوار الشمس ونور القمر ونور السراج مختلفة بالحقيقة مشتركة في عارض النور المطلق»؛ «فما مثاله»؛ «في الكل»؛ «عن الواجب والممكن».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The light of the sun, the light of the moon and the light of a lamp differ in reality, yet [it is asserted that] they share the accident or common predicate of “light” without qualification. This supplies the proposed analogy for predicating existence of diverse instances. “In all” means: in the Necessary as well as in the possible. The force of the comparison depends on whether that shared light is an independent accident or a common intelligible description; the printed discussion does not permit those alternatives to be silently identified.","source_image":"images/I046.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I046.upper.02","volume":1,"page":46,"physical_pdf_page":59,"layer":"upper","title":"The Ashʿarī attribution","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(نفس الماهية)»؛ «كما نسب إلى الشيخ الأشعري رحمه الله»؛ «وكأنه لهذا قال المعدوم ليس شيئاً».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Another view makes existence **the very quiddity itself**, a position here attributed to Shaykh al-Ashʿarī—may God have mercy on him. The text suggests that this is why he said that “the nonexistent is not a thing.” The claim is reported as an attribution in the source, not assumed to settle every dispute about what the Ashʿarī school itself teaches.","source_image":"images/I046.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I047.lower.01","volume":1,"page":47,"physical_pdf_page":60,"layer":"lower","title":"The critic on existence as an intellectual meaning","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله كأنهم قالوا ... الخ)»؛ «الوجود الصدري الاعتباري»؛ «الماهية»؛ «ليس في الخارج تحقق واحد»؛ «الوجود الاستحقاق» [last printed phrase uncertain].","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss says that some of the reported positions about the nonexistent may have arisen from a failure to distinguish **considered existence** from independently actual existence. One may grant a quiddity mental establishment in order to think about it without thereby positing its actuality in the external world. But the objection remains: if existence is regarded as an added feature, exactly where does the alleged addition take place? If there is no separately actualized existence, one cannot explain the doctrine by making the non-existent quiddity into a real extramental subject already prepared to receive an accident. [An expression near the first lines of the gloss is unclear; the argument is retained provisionally without supplying a conjectural reading.]","source_image":"images/I047.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I047.lower.02","volume":1,"page":47,"physical_pdf_page":60,"layer":"lower","title":"Restriction of “one realization”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فإن نفاه نفسه)»؛ «تحقق هو الموجود لا هو والوجود بتحققين»؛ «أم حاصلها أن المقصود كون وجود الشيء هو أنه إذا عرض الوجود على الماهية»؛ «لم يحصل هناك موجود واحد بوجود واحد»؛ «الاتصاف بالوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Saying that the thing and its existence do not have two distinct external realizations does not mean that the **concept of existence** is the **concept of quiddity**. Nor should it be construed as the claim that an independently existing accident and its independent substrate have fused into one. The objector illustrates the problem: if one says that existence comes to a quiddity, and a single existent then results, one has already smuggled in the idea of the pre-existing quiddity and of something additional. If existence itself is only the intellectual relation used in judgement, it cannot do the causal work that a real principle of effects would have to do. The margin follows this question across to I:48.","source_image":"images/I047.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I047.upper.01","volume":1,"page":47,"physical_pdf_page":60,"layer":"upper","title":"The contrast of the positions","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن الوجود إذا كان نفس الماهية فإذا ارتفع ارتفعت»؛ «فكان المعدوم نفياً صرفاً»؛ «بخلاف ما إذا قيل بأنه زائد عليها كالمعتزلة»؛ «فإن ارتفاعه لا يلزم ارتفاعها»؛ «فإن ارتفاع العارض لا يستلزم ارتفاع المعروض».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"For, if existence is the very quiddity, then when existence is removed, the quiddity itself is removed. The nonexistent is then sheer negation. That is unlike the view of those who say existence is additional to quiddity, such as the Muʿtazila: the removal of existence does not necessarily remove quiddity, just as the removal of an accident does not necessarily remove that in which it inheres. This is the reason that some of them affirm that the nonexistent is a thing, or possesses establishment [of a qualified sort]. The text is reporting the connection between the two theses, not assuming that everyone who calls existence additional must accept extramentally existent nonexistent things.","source_image":"images/I047.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I047.upper.02","volume":1,"page":47,"physical_pdf_page":60,"layer":"upper","title":"Not numerical identity of the concepts","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(في المعنى) أي المفهوم من الوجود هو المفهوم من الشيء»؛ «(يعني أنه لا ينفرد كل) من الوجود والماهية»؛ «(بتحقق على حدة في الخارج)»؛ «(وأما هو) أي ليس الشيء هوية ولعارضه المسمى بالوجود هوية أخرى».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"“In meaning” could be taken to imply that what is understood by existence is the same as what is understood by the thing; but that is not what is intended. Rather, existence and quiddity do not **each possess a distinct realization outside the mind**. There is not a quiddity with one extramental identity and an additional accident called existence with another extramental identity. Nor does the rejection of two identities make the notions synonymous; it is precisely the difference between conceptual distinction and external separation that matters here.","source_image":"images/I047.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.lower.01","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"lower","title":"The intention behind “I say ...”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله أقول الخ) حاصله أن ما ذكر مبني على أن المراد من الوجود هو الأمر الاعتباري»؛ «أما لو أريد به ما به التحقق فلا حاجة في القول بعينية الوجود للماهية»؛ «دور الماهية»؛ «القول بأنه اعتباري».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The marginal writer says the previous difficulty arises if “existence” is taken as a **considered or relational item**. But if the intended meaning is what makes something genuinely actual, then the statement that existence is identical with the thing's quiddity requires a different treatment. It is not enough to repeat that “existence is additional,” since the sense of the addition is precisely at issue. Nor does the rejection of an additional independently existing feature establish that the two ideas—existence and whatness—mean exactly the same thing.","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.lower.02","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"lower","title":"Two senses of predicating existence of a quiddity","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله ولأنت الخ)»؛ «(قوله بالإضافة إلى ذات الشيء وحقيقته)»؛ «إذا قيل للإنسان مثلا وجود في الخارج ووجود في الذهن»؛ «الوجودين»؛ «البياض والجسم»؛ «الإنسان وحقيقته».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"The gloss asks how to interpret the statement that the same quiddity has both **external existence** and **mental existence**. One must not imagine that there are necessarily two separately real entities called “the human being” merely because the human being can be conceived in the mind and realized outside it. Nor does the analogy with whiteness and body establish that mental existence is a further accident borne by a pre-existing thing in precisely the same way as an extramental colour is borne by a body. Rather, both the identity and the difference of the two modes must be explained: external actuality belongs to the thing in the world, while a mental form realizes the content for the knower. This resolves part of the equivocation without turning the individual quiddity itself into a universal that lives outside the mind.","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.lower.03","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"lower","title":"The literal gloss continues into I:49","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله بل الموجود في اللفظ الخ)»؛ «الوجود في اللفظ»؛ «الوجود في الخط»؛ «حقيقة الإنسان».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"When the text moves to “what exists in the word,” the commentator warns that the expression is not univocal. Spoken and written signs exist externally as sounds and traces, but the human quiddity designated by them does not thereby exist **as that human** within the sound or inscription. The precise remaining argument begins again on I:49; the continuation should be joined with that page during the later literal proof. [Final short printed words on the lower margin need reinspection; this is an intentionally bounded provisional unit.]","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed commentary","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.upper.01","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"upper","title":"The sense of existence relevant to effects","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أقول أنا لم أرد بالوجود المعنى النسبي الذي لا تحقق له في الخارج وإنما أريد به ما هو مبدأ الآثار وهو الهوية»؛ «ولا شك أنه بهذا المعنى نفس الشيء بلا تحقق» [final phrase imperfectly legible].","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"I say: I did not intend by “existence” the **relative meaning** which has no independent external realization. I intended that which is the *principle of effects*: the actual reality or identity of the thing. There is no doubt that existence in this sense is the thing itself. This is different from the abstract relational predicate which can be asserted of it; the author is identifying actuality in the relevant sense with the very existent, not with a second accident alongside it.","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.upper.02","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"upper","title":"External and mental existence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(ثم الوجود منقسم إلى العيني)»؛ «وهو الوجود المتأصل المقصود عليه الذي به تتحقق ذات الشيء ... في الخارج»؛ «(وإلى الذهني)»؛ «وجود غير متأصل بمنزلة الظل للجسم»؛ «والصورة المطابقة للشيء عيني إنها لو تحققت في الخارج لكانت ذلك الشيء».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Existence is then divided into **external** and **mental**. External existence is primary or original existence, by which a thing's essence obtains in the external world—indeed, it is the external realization of that essence. Mental existence is non-primary, resembling the relation of a body's **shadow** to the body. What is present in the mind is a form corresponding to the external thing: were the form itself actualized outside the mind, it would be that thing. Likewise, if a body's shadow were to become a body [in the proposed analogy], it would be that body's counterpart. This analogy does not equate the mental form with a separately existent person or object.","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I048.upper.03","volume":1,"page":48,"physical_pdf_page":61,"layer":"upper","title":"Word, writing and the kind of reality intended","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(حقيقة)»؛ «(و) منقسم (إلى اللفظي والخطي مجازاً)»؛ «(إذ ليس الموجود في اللفظ والخط من الإنسان)»؛ «(الشخص)»؛ «(ولا الماهية)»؛ «(في الخارج)»؛ «(في الذهن)»؛ «(بل)».","arabic_status":"selected_locating_anchors_not_full_transcription","english":"Mental existence can be called genuine with respect to the thing's quiddity and its reality as apprehended. “Verbal” and “written” existence, by contrast, are **figurative** modes of speaking about the existence of the thing: what exists in the spoken or inscribed word “human” is neither an external human individual nor the quiddity present as an intelligible form in the mind. What exists in speech is an utterance; what exists in writing is an inscription. These signify the thing but are not themselves that thing. The last printed “rather ...” continues at I:49, where the relation of the sign to the signified is clarified.","source_image":"images/I048.png","reading_status":"provisional_source_facing_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":75,"notes":["Printed page examined manually; marginal English remains partly interpretative; no independent second proof; not a complete page."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I049.lower.01","volume":1,"page":49,"physical_pdf_page":62,"layer":"lower","title":"the inscription may point to words without being words","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله نعم لو أضيف إلى اللفظ الموضوع الخ)» · «الوجود اللفظي المجازي» · «الوضع» · «النقش».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On the remark that the name might be added to the expression established by linguistic convention, the marginal writer clarifies the difference between the *conventional association* that makes an utterance significant and the *graphic inscription* that reproduces its shape. A written word is related to a spoken word, but the writing's existence on paper is not the spoken sound's existence in the air. It is a derivative or figurative mode of the word's availability to the reader.","source_image":"source_images/I049.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I049.lower.02","volume":1,"page":49,"physical_pdf_page":62,"layer":"lower","title":"does the representation preserve its object's identity?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الألفاظ» · «الصور الذهنية» · «الأعيان الخارجية» · «اختلاف الأوضاع والأشخاص».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The discussion examines a chain: object in external existence, the form by which one apprehends it, a spoken expression and a written mark. The links may be used to identify an object, but the identity of the thing represented must not be confused with identity of the representations. In particular, the same written or spoken name can refer to different particulars, whereas different names may indicate one referent. Consequently, one cannot demonstrate mental existence simply by asserting that the image and external object must share the same individual existence.","source_image":"source_images/I049.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I049.lower.03","volume":1,"page":49,"physical_pdf_page":62,"layer":"lower","title":"information about a nonexistent object","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الحكم على الأشياء» · «الصورة الحاصلة في الذهن» · «الأمور المعدومة» · «العلم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator then draws attention to judgement about absent or nonexistent objects. A person may acquire information about a thing not before the senses, because the concept or form is available for intellectual consideration. Yet the availability of this concept requires explanation: does it give the quiddity another mode of being, or does it supply a sign or likeness capable of sustaining judgement? The author indicates that the answer determines whether the opponent has proved the thesis of mental existence or only the more modest fact of conceiving what is absent.\n\n**Incomplete:** the last lower sentence breaks at the next leaf and its distinctive grammatical example needs exact retranscription.","source_image":"source_images/I049.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I049.upper.01","volume":1,"page":49,"physical_pdf_page":62,"layer":"upper","title":"from name to written mark to the thing signified","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(الاسم)» · «(و) في الخط (صورته) المرقومة» · «لو أضيف إلى اللفظ الموضوع» · «الوجود في الأعيان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"[The author completes a comparison involving the] **name** and its representation **in writing**, the inscribed shape of the expression. If the word signifies something, an inscribed form can signify the word, and through it lead us to what the word designates. But the ink or written pattern is not numerically identical with its spoken expression or the external object. The different relations of signification need to be distinguished from the actual mode of existence of the object signified.","source_image":"source_images/I049.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I049.upper.02","volume":1,"page":49,"physical_pdf_page":62,"layer":"upper","title":"the mental status of what cognition is about","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(والدليل على) الوجود (الذهني)» · «مع أن كون العلم سواء» · «في الأعيان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The argument for **mental existence** draws upon the fact that an object can be known and judged concerning even when the external thing is not present. It is possible to form an intelligible object of cognition, but the inference from that possibility to the **real presence of the object's quiddity in the mind** needs to be tested. Merely having a sign, or even a mental image, does not yet establish the exact metaphysical thesis.","source_image":"source_images/I049.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I050.lower.01","volume":1,"page":50,"physical_pdf_page":63,"layer":"lower","title":"the claim need not be that the identical external object exists mentally","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله مقتضيا الثبوت الخ)» · «الوجود الذهني» · «ملاحظة» · «لا حاجة إلى الدليل».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The lower commentator objects that the proof may establish no more than a meaningful presentation of a non-external object. The mind can attend to the subject and predicate and judge their relation. This does not by itself show that the very same quiddity, with all the attributes of an external existent, has migrated into another receptacle. Without distinguishing conceptual availability from extra-mental actuality, one risks making the conclusion true by definition rather than establishing it by demonstration.","source_image":"source_images/I050.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I050.lower.02","volume":1,"page":50,"physical_pdf_page":63,"layer":"lower","title":"the phoenix and the proposed 'mental subject'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«العنقاء» · «موجودا في الذهن» · «لم يكن حاصلا في الخارج» · «الوجود الذهني».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The author's reply is that a concept of the phoenix is possessed by the knower, and a judgement may be made with respect to it. But the mental concept is not thereby shown to have all the external predicates of a creature. In particular, saying “the phoenix is an animal” does not make a phoenix breathe in the room. The discussion is thus about the scope of predication and the dependence of cognition on a representation, not about a straightforward instance of an external relation.\n\n**Incomplete:** the final lower argumentative chain includes a difficulty about the subject–predicate relation; its intermediate clauses have been condensed.","source_image":"source_images/I050.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I050.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":50,"physical_pdf_page":63,"layer":"ruled","title":"affirmative predication is not an ordinary external inherence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله إذ نحكم على الممتنعات الخ)» · «الحكم في السلب» · «ثبوت».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The separately marked note qualifies the inference from affirmative judgement: an affirmative proposition involving an impossible subject is not the same as the external inherence of an accident in an existing substance. The intellect may combine concepts in a judgement; the ontological status of the object of that judgement remains the matter to be demonstrated.","source_image":"source_images/I050.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I050.upper.01","volume":1,"page":50,"physical_pdf_page":63,"layer":"upper","title":"the intellect can affirm what is not externally possible","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(أنا نعقل الأمور الثبوتية في الخارج إذ نحكم على الممتنعات)» · «إيجابا» · «الممتنع» · «من المفهومات».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The intellect forms concepts and even makes **affirmative judgements about impossibilities**. An object of judgement can therefore be present to thought without being an external thing. If the judgement contains a positive predicate, it is not enough simply to say that a positive judgement requires its subject to exist externally, since the very instances under discussion are said to be impossible externally. What exists in the mind may be the concept or representation under which the judgement is made.","source_image":"source_images/I050.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I050.upper.02","volume":1,"page":50,"physical_pdf_page":63,"layer":"upper","title":"the possible and impossible under a concept","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(ولا)» · «لا وجود لموضوعها في الخارج أصلا» · «كقولنا كل عنقاء حيوان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Consider a proposition about a phoenix: “Every phoenix is an animal.” The subject-expression can be understood and the predicate related to it in judgement although no phoenix need exist outside the intellect. The argument demands an explanation of what, exactly, is the subject of that predicate. Treating the conceptual subject as a presently existing external animal would be to misunderstand the example.","source_image":"source_images/I050.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I051.lower.01","volume":1,"page":51,"physical_pdf_page":64,"layer":"lower","title":"what is meant by 'the intelligible is present'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله والفهم والتميز عند العقل الخ)» · «وجوده في الخارج» · «وجوده في الذهن» · «الوجود المجرد».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The longer gloss explains that *intelligible presence* need not mean actual presence of the external subject. The mind may understand a proposition about a thing absent from the senses. If the thesis is only that a concept is present in cognition, it will hardly be disputed; if it is that the quiddity itself obtains a genuine additional form of existence, the argument needs premises that discriminate the two. The author considers a reply that the same quiddity can exist in different modes, while its externally associated accidents need not follow it into the mind.","source_image":"source_images/I051.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I051.lower.02","volume":1,"page":51,"physical_pdf_page":64,"layer":"lower","title":"a claimed additional existence and the danger of circularity","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«حاصل تجرد» · «الوجود في الذهن» · «الإضافة بين العاقل والمعقول» · «لا إثبات في الخارج».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss examines whether the relation of knower and known can be established before the mental presence of the known object is proved. If the relation is used as evidence for mental existence, one cannot simply define its terminus as a mentally existent instance of the object; that would assume the conclusion. Yet some relation appears involved in the cognitive act. The issue is whether its terminus must be an externally existing thing, an existent mental form, or an object given through the mind's intellectual act. The author's reply remains conditional on the interpretation of cognition.","source_image":"source_images/I051.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I051.lower.03","volume":1,"page":51,"physical_pdf_page":64,"layer":"lower","title":"ending of the proof and transition to the objection","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«حصول الصورة» · «الثبوت في الخارج» · «المعقول» · «الوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The marginal passage closes by requiring the reader to distinguish the existence of a *form of what is known* from the external actuality of the *thing itself*. The proponent regards the mental form as supplying the predicate-relation; the opponent may grant the form without granting what the proponent wishes to infer about the known object. The objection on I:52 will ask why, if the real object's essence is mentally present, it does not undergo all the same qualifications it would undergo in the external world.\n\n**Incomplete:** several lower sentences concerning the source of the cognitive relation require their original sequence reconstructed without condensation.","source_image":"source_images/I051.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I051.upper.01","volume":1,"page":51,"physical_pdf_page":64,"layer":"upper","title":"only an affirmative relation, not an externally subsisting accident, is required","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«كل جسم حادث والقضية» · «فالتعقل» · «(إن كان بالمحمول)» · «في الذهن» · «إضافة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"In a judgement such as “Every body is originated,” the intellect understands the subject and the predicate and affirms their relation. But the **act of understanding** is not identical with the external coming-to-be of each subject to which the judgement applies. If the relevant relation is obtained through the predicate's belonging to the subject conceptually, one must explain whether that belonging is an external accident or the relation employed by the mind in judging.","source_image":"source_images/I051.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I051.upper.02","volume":1,"page":51,"physical_pdf_page":64,"layer":"upper","title":"predication and addition are not the same","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ولا يكن محصله فيه» · «(فلا محالة يقتضي)» · «التعقل (إضافة)» · «بين العاقل والمعقول».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A proposed proof says that cognition requires a kind of **relation** (*iḍāfa*) between a knower and what is known. In the absence of the known thing externally, this relation must have a terminus in the mind. The respondent demands clarification: does the claim concern the existence of the known object itself, the presence of a concept through which it is known, or simply the logical relation expressed by the proposition? These are different claims, and their differences matter to the argument for mental existence.","source_image":"source_images/I051.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I052.lower.01","volume":1,"page":52,"physical_pdf_page":65,"layer":"lower","title":"forms need not be mental instances of every non-external object","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله قد يحصل المثال الخ)» · «المعقولات التي لا وجود لها في الخارج» · «صورة المعقول» · «العقل الفعال».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator cites a discussion in the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* about intelligibles that do not exist externally. A concept of a nonexistent or impossible thing is not a reason to suppose that the object itself has an external instance. The theory that mental forms are impressed or supplied through an intellectual cause must also explain how incompatible or impossible objects can be known without requiring those things to be realised. The objection is a problem for a simplistic account of mental existence, not a demonstration that there can be no representation of nonexistents.","source_image":"source_images/I052.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I052.lower.02","volume":1,"page":52,"physical_pdf_page":65,"layer":"lower","title":"how the known form can be present without inhering as the thing's external nature","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«صور الكائنات في العقل الفعال» · «الصفات» · «الوجود في الأعيان» · «المفهومات».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The author distinguishes the being of a form **in an intellect** from the thing's existence among external individuals. An intelligible can be available to the mind without the mind being an instance of every property predicated of the object. If one says the form is present as an intelligible, that requires no such external inherence; but if one says that the object itself is reproduced there in the complete manner of its external existence, the absurdities alleged by the opponent return. The debate continues at I:53 with the question whether the mental form and what the form depicts are identical.\n\n**Incomplete:** the dense lower text contains additional argument about the Active Intellect and mental conjunction that has not been translated word for word. The printed lower author's individual identity remains unassigned.","source_image":"source_images/I052.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I052.upper.01","volume":1,"page":52,"physical_pdf_page":65,"layer":"upper","title":"the familiar paradox of contradictory external properties","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«إذا قد حصل الأمر المطلوب بحصول المثال هذا» · «لو كان المعقول وجود» · «في الذهن» · «الأعراض المتصورة حتى المتضادات».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The opponent asks: if the actual object known really comes to exist in the mind, why do the accidents ordinarily associated with the object not arise there? Mental representations can include **contrary descriptions** or things whose external conjunction is impossible. Yet the mind does not thereby become the site of incompatible external qualities. Therefore the argument must make clear what “mental existence” means, rather than transferring the rules of external inherence into cognition without qualification.","source_image":"source_images/I052.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I052.upper.02","volume":1,"page":52,"physical_pdf_page":65,"layer":"upper","title":"the representation is present without the external attribute inhering","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(وهو وجود غير متأصل)» · «(لا يقتضي الاتصاف)» · «كالكون يتصور الكفر».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The response is that mental existence is **not the same kind of rooted, independent external existence**. Thus the mind's conceiving a quality does not entail that the mind becomes externally qualified by that quality. The one who conceives disbelief does not thereby become an unbeliever, just as someone may conceive a colour or a contradiction without inheriting the external attribute described. The consequence at issue is limited to apprehension, not to the occurrence of the objects' real physical properties in the knower.","source_image":"source_images/I052.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I052.upper.03","volume":1,"page":52,"physical_pdf_page":65,"layer":"upper","title":"water in a house versus water in a mind","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(كالماء في البيت)» · «بواسطة الكون» · «وجود الشيء في وجود أصيل».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A comparison is drawn with water in a house. The water's occupying a room is an externally realised spatial relation: its actual location in that room accompanies a particular condition of existence. When the *water* is apprehended by the mind, this does not mean that the very same water now occupies a corner of the brain as it occupies the house. The difference between external emplacement and intellectual representation explains why external attributes are not all transferred into cognition.","source_image":"source_images/I052.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I053.lower.01","volume":1,"page":53,"physical_pdf_page":66,"layer":"lower","title":"the narrower character of the mental-existence agreement","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فإن الكل متفق في الصورة الخ)» · «الفلاسفة» · «الصورة الحاصلة في الذهن» · «حقيقة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “All agree concerning the form”: the glossator observes that this common ground does not remove the disagreement. The philosophers' claim of union between the intelligible form and what is known may be meant in the level of quiddity or truth, whereas those denying mental existence understand the known form as a description or representation. If the union is stated without fixing the relevant respect, a merely verbal accord conceals a substantive ontological divergence. One must ask whether it is the genuine quiddity, or only something resembling it, which becomes present in understanding.","source_image":"source_images/I053.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I053.lower.02","volume":1,"page":53,"physical_pdf_page":66,"layer":"lower","title":"philosophers, theologians and the relation between the form and its object","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«هل هي عين ذي الصورة» · «وحدتها كماله عليه أهل التحقيق» · «الوجود الذهني» · «الوجود الحقيقي».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The marginal author compares the accounts attributed to philosophers, verifying scholars and those who deny the identity. The questions are whether a mental form possesses the same essence as its external object, whether mental and external existence are two manners of realizing one quiddity, and whether the form is only an analogical representation. If the form is merely another thing, then the object known may remain absent; if the object itself is asserted to be present, it becomes necessary to distinguish its mental mode of presence from the properties of its external occurrence.","source_image":"source_images/I053.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I053.lower.03","volume":1,"page":53,"physical_pdf_page":66,"layer":"lower","title":"'everything established is existent' and its dialectical limits","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فكل ما هو موجود معدوم الخ)» [opening uncertain] · «الوجود والثبوت والعدم» · «عندهم» · «الواسطة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The lower gloss reports that theologians differ over whether nonexistents can have a fixed status that is not existence. One camp holds the division between existence and nonexistence to be exhaustive. Another proposes the so-called fixed nonexistent, arguing from the meaningfulness of distinctions and judgements about absent things. The author's challenge is to say what this supposed middle rank adds to conceptual differentiation without simply renaming a mode of existence. [The final objection continues on I:54.]","source_image":"source_images/I053.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I053.upper.01","volume":1,"page":53,"physical_pdf_page":66,"layer":"upper","title":"what is being disputed about the mental form","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الخلاف في الوجود الذهني بالقطع» · «الكل متفق في الصورة» · «القائلين بالوجود الذهني» · «باتحاد الصورة وذي الصورة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The disagreement concerns **mental existence**. Everyone, it is said, accepts the presence of a *form* in cognition; the proponents of mental existence maintain that the form and what it is a form of are united under the relevant consideration. The opponents do not accept that identity. The issue is not whether anyone ever thinks of things, but whether the thing known is itself present mentally or whether a form distinct from it supplies the act of cognition.","source_image":"source_images/I053.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I053.upper.02","volume":1,"page":53,"physical_pdf_page":66,"layer":"upper","title":"being, fixity and the denial of intermediates","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ثم المعقول من الوجود والشيئية» · «الثبوت» · «ضرورة فكل ما هو ثابت فهو شيء وموجود وبالعكس» · «(و) المعقول (من العدم)».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"It follows that, on the account being defended, **existence, thinghood and establishment** are not three independently distinct ontological ranks. Whatever is established is a thing and is existent, and conversely. The notion signified by *nonexistence* is the contrary of existence; consequently one cannot assume an unargued middle state in which the object is at once absolutely nonexistent and yet positively established as a thing.","source_image":"source_images/I053.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I054.lower.01","volume":1,"page":54,"physical_pdf_page":67,"layer":"lower","title":"logical intermediates and real intermediates are not interchangeable","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«يلزم الترادف» · «(قوله والنزاع ... الخ)» · «الثبوت» · «النفي» · «الوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The glossator asks whether denying an intermediate level forces existence and positive fixity to be completely synonymous. Even if they are equivalent in extension under the disputed account, it need not follow that every use of their words has precisely the same meaning. The proposed intermediate rank may also conceal an ambiguity: an object can be known, named or considered mentally while being nonexistent externally, but none of these facts alone supplies a third ontological condition between existence and nothingness.","source_image":"source_images/I054.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I054.lower.02","volume":1,"page":54,"physical_pdf_page":67,"layer":"lower","title":"blackness, whiteness and the basis of an affirmative predication","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله إذ هو بحيث يتلوه الخ)» · «اللفظ» · «حقيقة في الوجود» · «المعدوم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The objection presses the interpretation of the opponent's examples. If the absolutely nonexistent can have the attribute of blackness or whiteness as an externally established property, the subject must be explained; if only the possibility of the predicate is intended, the argument does not establish the third rank. A difference among words, among conceived predicates, or among possible future instances must be distinguished from an actual difference among external entities.","source_image":"source_images/I054.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I054.upper.01","volume":1,"page":54,"physical_pdf_page":67,"layer":"upper","title":"the refusal of positive fixity for absolute nonexistence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«شيء ولا ثابت» · «(فكذا المعدوم ليس بشيء ولا ثابت)» · «المعدوم في الخارج» · «بالوجود الذهني».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—[The argument denies that the nonexistent can be a thing in the relevant sense or positively fixed.] Thus “the nonexistent is neither a thing nor established.” Admittedly, something nonexistent in **external** reality can be the subject of a judgement in the **mind**, on the theory of mental existence. But this does not make the absolutely nonexistent an existing thing outside the intellect. The dispute concerns the type of establishment claimed for it.","source_image":"source_images/I054.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I054.upper.02","volume":1,"page":54,"physical_pdf_page":67,"layer":"upper","title":"the argument for an intermediate rank","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(لا واسطة بينهما)» · «بين الوجود والعدم» · «(ومنهم من أثبتها)» · «المعدوم» · «السواد».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The author first denies any intermediate state between existence and nonexistence, then reports those who **affirm such a middle condition**. They say that an object may be *established* although not existent, so that the affirmation of a predicate may be made concerning something that has not yet been externally realised. The argument is tested with predicates like blackness and with the supposed fixed nature of a nonexistent possible thing.","source_image":"source_images/I054.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I055.lower.01","volume":1,"page":55,"physical_pdf_page":68,"layer":"lower","title":"the regress objection depends on a particular interpretation of qualification","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله أو ما يصدق عليه الخ)» · «التفسير» · «اتصاف» · «عدم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The lower commentator argues that the regress arises under a specific interpretation of a subject's receiving existence. It may be true that an externally subsisting accident requires a subject, but the controversial question is whether existence itself is just another accident of this sort. If the subject has to exist prior to its being qualified as existent, the account becomes circular; if not, the meaning of the attribution needs to be distinguished from ordinary inherence. The explanatory terms cannot be substituted for one another without argument.","source_image":"source_images/I055.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I055.lower.02","volume":1,"page":55,"physical_pdf_page":68,"layer":"lower","title":"why the other side also fails if it simply makes nonexistence a positive attribute","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله وأنا نقضه اللاوجود)» [reading uncertain] · «لا يصح» · «نقيض» · «الثبوت والعدم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The glossator tests the opposing account: if nonexistence is made a positive attribute, a nonexistent subject will have to obtain the attribute in some way, and the same puzzles recur. But if nonexistence means simply the absence of existence, there is no need to imagine a further quality inhering in a fixed object. The difference between negating a predicate and affirming an opposite positive property remains indispensable.","source_image":"source_images/I055.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I055.lower.03","volume":1,"page":55,"physical_pdf_page":68,"layer":"lower","title":"blackness and whiteness as misleading analogies","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«السواد والبياض» · «الصفات الثبوتية» · «الصفات السلبية» · «لهم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Blackness and whiteness are positive qualities that can belong to a body. Nonexistence, by contrast, cannot be placed in the same class merely because the grammar of a proposition allows it to be predicated. A subject can be described as not black without acquiring a new positive accident called *not-blackness*. Similarly, to say that something does not exist may deny its being rather than give it another positive quality. This helps expose the difference between contradictories, contraries and the denial of an attribute.","source_image":"source_images/I055.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I055.upper.01","volume":1,"page":55,"physical_pdf_page":68,"layer":"upper","title":"existence as an attribute of an already established subject","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قال)» · «يجعل الوجود صفة إذا وجد تسلسل» · «الوجودات» · «أو عدم اتصاف بالنقيض».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"“They say”: if existence is treated as an additional positive attribute which must itself become existent, then one seems required to posit another existence for it, and so on without end. Alternatively, if nonexistence is said to be simply the subject's being qualified by a contradictory predicate, the account must explain how the subject is present in order to receive that contradictory qualification.","source_image":"source_images/I055.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I055.upper.02","volume":1,"page":55,"physical_pdf_page":68,"layer":"upper","title":"replies to the regress and to the paradox of the subject's being absent","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(ورد)» · «أنه موجود» · «(لا أن وجوده عينه)» · «(عدم)».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The reply notes that an account of existence as an attribute need not grant that every attribution has the same form as an accident inhering in a substance. Without first resolving what it means for the subject to be there, one cannot pass immediately to an infinite series. Likewise, speaking of an object's nonexistence does not necessarily establish an independently existent contradictory accident in that object. These are competing explanations of one and the same predication.","source_image":"source_images/I055.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.lower.01","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"lower","title":"the two formulae 'existence is not there' and 'the nonexistent is there'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«اللاوجود عند القائلين بالواسطة» · «المعدوم عند المثبتين» · «عند النافين» · «العدم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The marginal commentator distinguishes the views of those who posit an intermediary rank from the views of those who deny one. The former may call the nonexistent *established* but not *existent*, allowing possibility and conceptual predication without external being. The latter refuse to grant anything outside the exhaustive pair existence/nonexistence. The gloss warns that both sides use the term “established” and the term “nonexistent” under varying specifications; to argue against one formulation while quietly using another is not a sound refutation.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.lower.02","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"lower","title":"being a subject of denial does not establish a subject in actuality","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(وأقول)» · «كان الوجود معدوما» · «لو كان حالا» · «كلا».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"“I say”: even if a possible thing receives the judgement that existence is absent from it, this does not force the existence of a substrate in which that absence subsists. The difference between the *subject as conceived* and the *subject as externally fixed* matters at every step. The gloss considers the objection that nonexistence must occur as a positive state and replies that doing so would contradict the very point of denying an extra-mental existent.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.lower.03","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"lower","title":"what does it mean to be an intermediate condition?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الواسطة» · «ليست موجودة ولا معدومة» · «لا يمكن» · «الوجود لا صفة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The alleged intermediary between the existent and the nonexistent may be said to be neither positively existent nor absolutely nonexistent. But if the intermediary itself is a subject of predication, the argument still owes an account of its ontological status. Is it an objective condition, a mental consideration, or a change in terminology? The commentator disputes whether the original formulation truly supplies a third alternative or only shifts the meaning of its terms.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.lower.04","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"lower","title":"the marginal author's cautious conclusion","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وهو بين البطلان» · «من شرح المقاصد» · «بطريق التفسير واللطف» · «تحقيقه من رسالتنا».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss finally suggests that much of the difficulty may arise from the way the rival schools explain their terms. The same surface expressions are employed differently by those who admit an intermediary and those who reject it. A reconciliation is possible only if one shows that their actual ontological claims coincide; otherwise the differences cannot be dissolved by linguistic adjustment alone. The writer refers to another, more detailed treatment of the point and closes the discussion before the illustration at I:57.\n\n**Still unresolved:** the bottom third of I:55 and most of the lower argumentative chain on I:56 need exact, uncompressed translation. These pages are **not** certified first-pass complete despite the connected reading above.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.upper.01","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"upper","title":"the alleged logical contradiction","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(و)» · «اتصافه بالنقيض بل بالمعدوم» · «(نقيضه العدم لا المعدوم)» · «بطرق الاشتقاق».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The dispute is sharpened by distinguishing **nonexistence** (*ʿadam*) from **the nonexistent thing** (*maʿdūm*). If the contradiction of existence is nonexistence, it does not follow that “the nonexistent” is the same sort of predicate. The latter is a derived description applied to a subject; the former expresses negation of being. Arguments that treat them as interchangeable may therefore depend on an unnoticed shift from an abstract contradictory to a derived adjectival predicate.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I056.upper.02","volume":1,"page":56,"physical_pdf_page":69,"layer":"upper","title":"why existence and nonexistence cannot be treated as two substances","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن كل صفة فائقة شيء» [source reading uncertain] · «الوجود» · «العدم» · «نقيض».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"An apparent paradox arises if both existence and nonexistence are handled as independently positive qualifications. The author asks whether an attribution of existence makes the subject independently existent beforehand, and whether attributing nonexistence does likewise. The regress and contradiction can be avoided only by fixing the relevant sense of qualification. The upper text ends by opposing the *actual negation of existence* to the supposedly positive *object described as nonexistent*.","source_image":"source_images/I056.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.lower.01","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"lower","title":"whether 'the body is a nonbody' follows","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله كالسواد الخ)» · «اتصاف الوجود بالعدم» · «لا يكون الوجود موجودا» · «لا عذر فيه».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The longer commentator returns to the proposed example: in a body the qualification by blackness leaves the subject's existence intact, but an attribution of nonexistence to existence is not of that sort. The relevant inference depends on what precisely the subject is said to lack. If the predicate negates the subject considered in its very identity, the analogy breaks down. The objection thus turns not on the grammar alone but on the difference between a separable or accidental predicate and a contradiction in the subject's being.","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.lower.02","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"lower","title":"does the formula 'not possible' differ from the absence of possibility?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله لعدم الفرق بين الإمكان له والإمكان لغيره الخ)» · «الإمكان» · «منفيا في نفسه» · «نفي ثبوته».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A further gloss distinguishes the negation of possibility from the possibility of negation, as well as “the thing has possibility” from “possibility is affirmed for something else.” The author stresses that an assertion involving the possible does not by itself make the possible object existent. Saying that a thing may exist is consistent with its presently not existing. One cannot, without another premise, move from the stability of a conceptual distinction to the established existence of its subject.","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"ruled","title":"the force of the blackness analogy","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله كالسواد الخ)» · «اتصاف الوجود بالعدم» · «اتصاف الجسم بالسواد» · «المعدوم في نفسه».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On the blackness analogy, the smaller gloss denies that the relation of existence to nonexistence can simply be treated as equivalent to the relation between a subject and the blackness inhering in it. To say that existence receives nonexistence may negate the very existence under discussion, whereas a body's receiving blackness does not negate its being a body. An appeal to an ordinary attribute therefore cannot settle a dispute over the contradictory of existence itself. The note also challenges the move from a description's being conceived to its object being established in itself.","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.upper.01","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"upper","title":"the blackness/body counterexample","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«كالسواد القائم بالجسم» · «لا جسم مع اتصاف الجسم به» · «الجسم ذو لا جسم» · «الوجود معدوم ولا وجود».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"[The disputed description could attach] as one of the individuals falling under its contradictory—just as blackness inheres in a body. Yet a body qualified by blackness is not, merely for that reason, a nonbody. The reductio is that if one treats the presence of a predicate as the thing's becoming the contrary of itself, one will be forced to say absurdly that the body, precisely while a body, possesses nonbodiness. In the same way, it would be mistaken to infer from a proposed way of speaking about existence that “this existence is nonexistent” or is no existence at all. The point is to test what the claimed opposition actually concerns, not to collapse the levels of predication.","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.upper.02","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"upper","title":"the differentiability of nonexistents","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قالوا)» · «المعدومات متمايزة» · «بعضها متصور وبعضها غير متصور» · «بعضها صادق وبعضها كاذب» · «ولا يعقل التميز بدون الثبوت».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"“They say”: Nonexistent things are **distinguishable from one another**. Some can be conceived and some cannot; some assertions concerning them are true and others false. But differentiation, the argument proceeds, is unintelligible without a kind of fixity or being-established (*ṯubūt*). For one can distinguish two items only insofar as they can be referred to, and reference appears to require something fixed as its object. Thus the opponents attempt to infer a kind of established status for the nonexistent from the fact that it can be thought and described.","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I057.upper.03","volume":1,"page":57,"physical_pdf_page":70,"layer":"upper","title":"from an object's possibility to its being established","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ولا يعقل التميز بدون الثبوت» · «(و) كل ممكن ثابت» · «الإمكان» · «ثبوت».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A second argument is introduced from possibility. Every possible thing, it is said, is “established” in respect of its possibility, even if it is not actually existent. Otherwise possibility and impossibility would have nothing to differentiate them before the thing's realization. This argument requires careful examination, since saying “it is possible that X exists” may be quite different from saying that X already possesses a positive ontological standing. [The final words lead into the objection on I:58.]","source_image":"source_images/I057.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I058.lower.01","volume":1,"page":58,"physical_pdf_page":71,"layer":"lower","title":"is the alleged difference already what is being disputed?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله يعني أن الفرق الخ)» · «وصف الإمكان بالشيء» · «بناء على اعتقاد الخصم» · «ليس أمرا موجودا في نفسه».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “That is, the difference ...”: the intended contrast concerns possibility as a description of a subject and possibility attributed to or denied of that subject. But the distinction does not prove itself. It may presuppose the opponent's thesis that there is a fixed subject in which possibility inheres. The one who denies such a subject can still distinguish a statement of possibility from its negation, because the two statements have different significations without requiring the independently existing thing whose possibility is disputed.","source_image":"source_images/I058.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I058.lower.02","volume":1,"page":58,"physical_pdf_page":71,"layer":"lower","title":"the substance of a negative proposition","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الإمكان في حد ذاته» · «منفيا عن الغير أيضا» · «سلب الإمكان» · «وصف ثبوتي».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator examines what it means to deny possibility “in itself.” If the possibility is a positive attribute, then denying it of another thing does not necessarily deny the very intelligibility of possibility; nor does its absence from some subject make it a nonentity absolutely. But if the dispute is about a nonexistent possible thing, it is precisely illicit to posit the fixed subject as part of the premise. The distinction between positive and negative descriptions must not beg the question of extramental being.","source_image":"source_images/I058.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I058.lower.03","volume":1,"page":58,"physical_pdf_page":71,"layer":"lower","title":"the form of the objection and the necessary reply","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ليس فرق بينها» · «التناقض» · «الفرق بين ماهيتين» · «قيل ... عدم الإمكان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss presses an alternative: perhaps the meaningful difference is only between affirming and denying an attribution, not between two ontological objects. If so, the opponent has shown a difference between statements, but has not established the subject in the manner required. The author considers the case in which denial of possibility is treated as a real property and the case in which it merely records that the asserted possibility is not available. The latter preserves the distinction without creating a new real existent.","source_image":"source_images/I058.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I058.lower.04","volume":1,"page":58,"physical_pdf_page":71,"layer":"lower","title":"why a different understanding of the subject changes the argument","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الممكن» · «في الأذهان» · «الموجود في الأعيان» · «ما لا يمكن».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"It remains open whether the intended subject is the *possible quiddity in the intellect*, a really existing external individual, or something claimed to be fixed while not existing. Those are not equivalent. The marginal author insists that, if the argument changes its sense of “subject” midway, the appearance of a proof from possibility to positive fixity disappears. This opens the next printed page, where the meaning of *thubūt* itself is disputed.","source_image":"source_images/I058.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I058.upper.01","volume":1,"page":58,"physical_pdf_page":71,"layer":"upper","title":"the proposed difference between possibility and its negation","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«للفرق بين الإمكان له ولا إمكان له» · «ثبوت الإمكان للشيء» · «وإن لم يكن موجودا في نفسه» · «(ثبت موصوفه)».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"[The point is alleged to be] the difference between “it is possible for it” and “it is not possible for it.” That is, possibility can be truly attributed to a thing even if the thing is not existent in itself. But it does not follow that the difference between the two judgements proves that the thing has an independently existing substrate of possibility. The proof's next step claims that, if the attribute of possibility is established, **its subject must be established**; the commentary asks in which sense the subject is being asserted to be established.","source_image":"source_images/I058.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.lower.01","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"lower","title":"the absolute nonexistent and the nonexistent-at-present","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فلا يفيد)» · «العدم المطلق» · «لا وجود له أصلا» · «معدوم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “It does not establish the conclusion”: the reason is that the discussion concerns the absolutely nonexistent, not a thing that once existed, or that exists somewhere else, or that is merely absent at the present instant. One must ask whether a thing's absence is absolute or relative to a particular time or description. An earlier or later existent can be spoken of as nonexistent now without becoming an absolutely nonexistent object.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.lower.02","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"lower","title":"the argument from being fixed in oneself","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله في نفسه)» · «الإمكان» · «غير ثابت أصلا» · «ثبوت معنى».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator explains that “in itself” may describe the status of a concept under consideration, not an independently established extra-mental entity. If possibility does not exist as an external quality, it is mistaken to infer that the possible object must be established externally. But if the possibility is considered as something intelligible, that does not prove the ontological claim either. The objection turns on precisely this ambiguity.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.lower.03","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"lower","title":"why the denial of nonexistence does not yield external existence","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله وإن أردت في الذهن فلا يفيد)» · «وجود الذهني» · «المعدوم» · «الإمكان المنفي».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"If the required establishment is only in the mind, the conclusion is not the one the opponent sought. The mind can hold a concept of something that has no external being. But if an external existence is required, an argument that appeals merely to differentiation among concepts cannot establish it. Thus the reply distinguishes external fixity, intellectual presence and the truth of a statement concerning the possible.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.upper.01","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"upper","title":"the external sense of existence is not simply being conceded","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قلنا)» · «أن (التميز) يقتضي الثبوت في الخارج» · «ممنوع» · «إنما يلزم أن لو كان بحسب الخارج».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"“We reply”: It is denied that the fact of differentiation requires establishment **in external existence**. That consequence would follow only if the difference were itself an external difference between external entities. A distinction made in reason or thought need not have two independently existent objects in the external world corresponding to its two terms. The distinction, therefore, proves less than its advocates require.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.upper.02","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"upper","title":"the being of a possible and of an impossible object","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«و(أما)» · «عند العقل» · «اجتماع النقيضين» · «المركبات الخيالية» · «وإن أردت في الذهن».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"At the level of intellect, one may differentiate objects even when they cannot exist externally, such as a combination of contradictories or a composite imagined only in thought. Such differentiation can be meaningful **in the understanding** while its object has no external existence. A thought can therefore distinguish and compare nonexistents without thereby establishing them as external things.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I059.upper.03","volume":1,"page":59,"physical_pdf_page":72,"layer":"upper","title":"the meaning of possibility's being fixed","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(وأن الفرق بين نفي الإمكان والإمكان المنفي)» · «في نفسه» · «على تقدير كونه منفيا» · «ثابت أيضا».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The alleged difference between denying possibility and possibility considered under negation is likewise a difference in what is affirmed or denied. Even if we grant a stable notion of possibility, that stability may belong to a conceptual judgement rather than to the existence of the possible thing. To infer an existent subject from the conceptual distinction would again be to pass illicitly from the mind to the external world.","source_image":"source_images/I059.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I060.lower.01","volume":1,"page":60,"physical_pdf_page":73,"layer":"lower","title":"preventing a slide from conceptual denial to real impossibility","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فلا يلزم من كونه غير ثبوت الخ)» · «إنما ليس من ذلك» · «الصفات الثبوتية والمنفية» · «سلب الشيء».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss explains that the intended reply is to distinguish the absence of a positive determination from the impossibility of thinking or predicating the thing. Negation may be genuine without itself being another existent positive property. One may deny that something is external or deny a feature of a subject while still entertaining a meaningful proposition concerning that subject. The author therefore resists a reading in which every meaningful negative predicate paradoxically becomes a positive attribute of an existent.","source_image":"source_images/I060.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I060.lower.02","volume":1,"page":60,"physical_pdf_page":73,"layer":"lower","title":"contrasting contradictory predicates with the subject's actual status","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الصفات الثبوتية والمنفية» · «الاعتبارات» · «قد يصدق أحدهما» · «كذب الأخرى».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The difference between contradictories can be expressed by the truth of one statement and falsity of its contrary, without supposing that the contrary is fixed as a second objective quality. Logical opposition belongs to propositions under a given interpretation. The commentary therefore asks whether the negative and affirmative predicates are being used in the same respect, at the same time, and of the same thing; unless these conditions are retained, an alleged contradiction may be only verbal.","source_image":"source_images/I060.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I060.lower.03","volume":1,"page":60,"physical_pdf_page":73,"layer":"lower","title":"the movement toward affirmative predication in I:61","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله كما في وجود زيد الخ)» · «الوجود الذهني» · «عدم» · «في الزمان» · «اللاحق».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The example of Zayd's existence illustrates the difference between an object's existence at a time and the judgement of its existence. A past or future existent can be treated as absent now, although the expression is not senseless. The later and earlier temporal qualifications must not be allowed to alter silently what the predicate *exists* is taken to mean. This prepares the question addressed on I:61: **what unites the two terms in a true affirmative proposition, and what distinguishes their concepts?**","source_image":"source_images/I060.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I060.upper.01","volume":1,"page":60,"physical_pdf_page":73,"layer":"upper","title":"why the possibility of a thing need not make the thing 'fixed'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فلا يلزم من كونه غير ثبوت سلب الإمكان عن الممكن» · «ثبوت الشيء لنفسه» · «الخارج» · «الممتنع».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"It does not follow from [a particular item's] not having positive fixity that possibility must be denied of the possible. Nor should the proposition “a thing is itself” be taken as proof that every conceivable thing is externally established. The identity at issue can be preserved in a predicative or conceptual judgement without requiring the independent existence of the subject in the manner claimed by the opposing argument.","source_image":"source_images/I060.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I060.upper.02","volume":1,"page":60,"physical_pdf_page":73,"layer":"upper","title":"judging a subject as existing, nonexistent or merely conceived","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(ثم كل من الوجود والعدم قد يقع محمولا)» · «وقد يقع رابطة» · «المحمول للموضوع» · «الحكم الإيجابي» · «وجود المحمول للموضوع».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"**Existence and nonexistence may each occur as predicates.** A judgement may also make use of a *link* or copula between subject and predicate. When existence is predicated, the judgement is about existence; when a judgement is affirmative with a different predicate, the existence of the predicate *for the subject* is not necessarily the same as the subject's external existence. The distinction is crucial in statements about nonexistent or imagined subjects: the truth or coherence of the predication cannot simply be reduced to the subject's independent external being.","source_image":"source_images/I060.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I061.lower.01","volume":1,"page":61,"physical_pdf_page":74,"layer":"lower","title":"why merely invoking the 'unity of the terms' does not answer the objection","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله يعني أن يكون ما يصدق عليه في الخارج الخ)» · «المراد اتحاد الطرفين» · «في الوجود الخارجي» · «للتغاير في المفهوم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator explains that the phrase “what the expression is true of externally” points back to the explanation of the unity of the two terms. If we take their unity to mean an external identity, that cannot simply be read as the identity of their meanings: the fact that two expressions attach to one existent does not show that their significations are interchangeable. Conversely, an identity secured only through a consideration of the intellect may fall short of the external union a predication is being used to affirm. This is an objection to an overly compressed account of what “unity” is doing in the definition.","source_image":"source_images/I061.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I061.lower.02","volume":1,"page":61,"physical_pdf_page":74,"layer":"lower","title":"the mind's judgement and the external case","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن اتحاد الطرفين ... في الخارج» · «الذهن» · «مع الوجود» · «مع اعتبار العدم» · «اعتزال ماهية المعلومة موجودة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"If the relation is taken in the external order, there must be an item there under both considerations; if it is taken in the mind, the question becomes whether the very same item can be regarded under incompatible conditions. The objection presses the asymmetry: a subject may be considered under existence, under nonexistence, or simply as a quiddity, but not all these considerations can be treated as independently existing properties of one extra-mental thing. A mental judgement may truly affirm a predicate of a quiddity without the relevant combination being realised in external existence.","source_image":"source_images/I061.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I061.lower.03","volume":1,"page":61,"physical_pdf_page":74,"layer":"lower","title":"why the subject may not be 'outside' yet remain an intelligible subject","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن الحكم على الذات» · «لا يلزم أن يعتبر فيه أحدهما» · «لأن الحكم على الذات من غير أن يعتبر معه شيء».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator goes on to reject an inference from an attribution made to the essence to the positive presence of every qualification required to formulate it. The essence is considered without adding an existential condition: ascribing the predicate to it does not force us to posit two real entities, the quiddity and a separate “being-there,” nor does denying existence turn the very subject of the judgement into absolute nothingness. These alternatives would only arise if one mistook a distinction in consideration for an external division.","source_image":"source_images/I061.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I061.upper.01","volume":1,"page":61,"physical_pdf_page":74,"layer":"upper","title":"final requirements for a true affirmative predication","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وافتقر الحمل الإيجابي إلى اتحاد الطرفين» · «ذاتا» · «وهوية» · «ما يصدق عليه في الخارج أو الذهن عنوان الموضوع» · «هو بعينه الذي يصدق عليه مفهوم المحمول».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—[The previous discussion concludes with what is required for predication.] An affirmative predication depends upon the two terms' being united **in respect of the thing itself and of its identity**. The meaning is that the item of which the subject-expression is true—whether externally or in the mind—is precisely the item of which the predicate-concept is true. A judgement of the form “this is that” is otherwise not warranted where the purported subject and predicate designate two distinct entities.","source_image":"source_images/I061.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I061.upper.02","volume":1,"page":61,"physical_pdf_page":74,"layer":"upper","title":"why difference of concepts is nevertheless required","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وإلى تغايرهما مفهوما» · «مفهوم عنوان الموضوع مغايرا لمفهوم المحمول» · «ليفيد» · «فائدة يعتد بها» · «وقد يكون فاسدا ... و باطلا».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Yet the concepts signified by the two expressions must also be different: the concept conveyed by the subject-expression must differ from the predicate-concept, **so that the predication conveys a genuine benefit or information**. The author's reasoning considers cases in which this requirement appears to fail, including judgements that are formally true but explanatorily pointless and judgements whose apparent agreement depends upon a merely mental consideration. Thus one must distinguish identity of the *referent* from identity of the *concepts*; to confuse those two identities is to obscure the purpose of affirmative predication. [The exact status of two short transitions in the closing upper lines requires a second reading.]","source_image":"source_images/I061.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I062.lower.01","volume":1,"page":62,"physical_pdf_page":75,"layer":"lower","title":"whether this account is applicable where there is no existing thing","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله والمراد بالأمر الشيء وبالنفس الذات الخ)» · «قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله» · «كيف يتصور هذا فيما لا ذات له ولا شيئية له في الأعيان» · «اجتماع النقيضين محال».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator introduces a passage from the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*: How can the expression be applied to what possesses neither an essence nor thinghood among external things? For we say that the conjunction of contradictories is impossible *in itself*, and that a statement about what is possible is or is not in accordance with what obtains in the thing itself. These claims are not restricted to an independently present external object. The suggested answer is that, upon considering two opposed terms, the intellect discerns an affirmative or negative relation between them—sometimes a necessary relation, sometimes one excluded by necessity, and sometimes one that may obtain or fail. The question is what makes that discernment correct, and whether it is independent of the particular act of judgement.","source_image":"source_images/I062.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I062.lower.02","volume":1,"page":62,"physical_pdf_page":75,"layer":"lower","title":"the two relata and the truth of their connection","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«العقل عند ملاحظة الطرفين» · «نسبة إيجابية أو سلبية» · «مقتضاها الضرورة أو البرهان» · «خصوصية المدرك والمخبر».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The relation is not exhausted by the fact that somebody conceived it or reported it. Once the parties to the judgement are considered, the mind may discover that one relation follows necessarily, or can be demonstrated, whereas an incompatible relation cannot follow. In this discussion “what is in itself” is therefore invoked to distinguish the **ground of a correct judgement** from the mere occurrence of its being judged. One must not confuse the relation's being intellectually apprehended with its being produced by an arbitrary act of apprehension.","source_image":"source_images/I062.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I062.lower.03","volume":1,"page":62,"physical_pdf_page":75,"layer":"lower","title":"whether all mental forms must be jointly present","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«صور جميع الكائنات والمعدومات» · «في العقل الفعال» · «لا يكون في نفس الأمر» · «الإيمان» · «الإذعان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A further possibility is reported: perhaps all the forms of existent and nonexistent things are present in the Active Intellect, and correspondence is correspondence with those forms. But this does not yet resolve what a true attribution consists in. An affirmation can be held with assent (*iḏʿān*), or presented merely as a conceived proposition; its truth cannot consist in the speaker's act of assent. The author distinguishes the mind's determination of the conceptual terms from the truth of the relation asserted between them. References to assent, existence in the intellect and external realization are not to be collapsed into one claim.","source_image":"source_images/I062.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I062.lower.04","volume":1,"page":62,"physical_pdf_page":75,"layer":"lower","title":"possible existence and the case of something thought but not realised","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أن يكون في نفس الأمر» · «في مرتبة الشيء وحد ذاته» · «في الأعيان أو يكون في الأذهان» · «إن لم يكن في الأعيان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The final argument returns to the thing's rank or essence, even where what is considered does not exist in external particulars. A quiddity can be conceived, and an essential relation to it can be correctly affirmed without thereby furnishing a corresponding external object. Conversely, a merely imagined relation is not established by the fact that it has been imagined. The discussion therefore separates **external being, mental presence, and truth or necessity belonging to a predicative relation**.","source_image":"source_images/I062.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I062.upper.01","volume":1,"page":62,"physical_pdf_page":75,"layer":"upper","title":"what is intended by 'the thing in itself'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ما في نفس الأمر» · «والمراد بالأمر الشيء وبالنفس الذات» · «في حد ذاته وبالنظر إليه» · «مع قطع النظر عن حكمنا» · «وإدراك المدرك».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"[Truth is discussed by appeal to] what obtains **in the thing itself** (*mā fī nafs al-amr*). By *amr* is meant “thing,” and by *nafs* “essence” or “self.” The intended sense is what we understand when we say, “This thing is such-and-such *in itself*”: that is, with regard to what it is, considered in its own identity, **disregarding our judgement about it and the apprehension of the person who apprehends it**. It does not follow that “in itself” designates a second external receptacle beyond the thing.","source_image":"source_images/I062.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.lower.01","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"lower","title":"the many senses of the term","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فصل في الماهية الخ)» · «إذا اعتبرت مع التحقق سميت ذاتا وحقيقة» · «وإذا اعتبرت مع التشخص سميت هوية» · «الوجود الخارجي».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “Section: On quiddity.” The commentator cites the *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* to explain that, when quiddity is considered with actuality, it may be called an essence (*ḏāt*) or reality (*ḥaqīqa*); when considered with individuation it is called a concrete identity (*huwiyya*). The term *identity* may also be applied to individuation itself or to external existence, and *essence* can designate the essence insofar as it is predicable of individuals. These are different **respects under which one quiddity is contemplated**, not automatically different objects in external existence.","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.lower.02","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"lower","title":"can quiddity be made up of all its accidents?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الماهية تعتبر جميع عوارضها لازمة أو مفارقة» · «الأمور العامة باطل» · «الأحوال والعوارض».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss contests a proposed way of gathering all concomitants into the definition of quiddity. If quiddity were understood as the totality of its necessary and separable accidents, every predicate would become constitutive of what the thing is, and the distinction between essence and what is predicated of the essence would collapse. That cannot be the meaning of the standard definition. The author examines what is necessary to the thing, what belongs to it by accident, and what can be set aside without destroying what is answered to the question “What is it?”","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.lower.03","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"lower","title":"what is signified by the word 'what'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الماهية ما صدقاتها» · «المفهوم الصادق» · «ما هو ذي ماهية» · «(قوله عن السؤال بما هو الخ)».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The expression *mā* in “What is it?” directs attention to an answer indicating an essence rather than to a bare occurrence of the word *thing*. But one must beware of construing the answer as nothing more than a name for the existing individual; an individual may bear many descriptions, none of which individually supplies the definition demanded. The question seeks the content by which the thing is what it is. The gloss therefore distinguishes what a general expression signifies, the instances of which it is true, and the essence that is intended by the definitional answer.","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.lower.04","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"lower","title":"definition, the intelligible, and signification","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«هو حد الشيء» · «ما يؤخذ منه الموصوفة» · «المقصود بالإشارة» · «هذا المحدد».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator further considers whether “quiddity” is being used for a complete definition, for the intelligible meaning delivered by such a definition, or for the thing to which it points. These may coincide in the act of successful definition, but must not be confused when disputing a definition's wording. In particular, the *meaning* intended by the question may be the same whether the corresponding thing happens to be externally present or not. The concluding transition leads to the different ways in which a quiddity can be taken or considered.","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.upper.01","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"upper","title":"the subject of the chapter","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فصل في الماهية» · «آخر مباحث ما بحث الوجود والعدم» · «لأن البحث عنهما من حيث إنهما صفتان للماهية معروضتان لها».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"**Section: On Quiddity.** It is placed after the discussions of existence and nonexistence, because inquiry into the latter two is an inquiry into them as descriptions attributed to quiddity, the thing of which those descriptions are predicated. To discuss existence and nonexistence in that manner presupposes an explanation of what is intended by the quiddity receiving the two descriptions.","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I063.upper.02","volume":1,"page":63,"physical_pdf_page":76,"layer":"upper","title":"what 'what is it?' asks for","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لفظة ما مأخوذة عن ما هو» · «ماهية الشيء ما يجاب به عن السؤال بما هو».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The word *māhiyya* (“quiddity”) is derived from the question *mā huwa* (“What is it?”). **The quiddity of a thing is what is given in reply to the question, “What is it?”** The requested answer identifies what the thing is rather than supplying a merely accidental circumstance associated with it. [The last phrase proceeds into I:64.]","source_image":"source_images/I063.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);